</pre><div></div><div>Working as expected!</div><div>OK, let''s move to the next step!</div><div></div><h2>Add a unit test pipe that will fail the whole process</h2><div></div><pre data-language="plain">name: Deploy Angular Blog to Cloud Run

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main 

jobs:
  test:
    name: Run Unit Tests
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v3

      - name: Set up Node.js
        uses: actions/setup-node@v3
        with:
          node-version: ''18''

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: npm ci

      - name: Run unit tests
        run: npm test

  deploy:
    name: Build and Deploy
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    needs: test
    
    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v3

      - name: Set up Node.js
        uses: actions/setup-node@v3
        with:
          node-version: ''18''

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: npm ci

      - name: Build Angular app
        run: npm run build --prod

      - name: Authenticate to Google Cloud
        uses: google-github-actions/auth@v2
        with:
          credentials_json: ''${{ secrets.GCP_SA_KEY }}''

      - name: Install Google Cloud SDK
        run: |
          sudo apt-get install -y apt-transport-https ca-certificates
          sudo apt-get update &amp;&amp; sudo apt-get install -y google-cloud-sdk
          gcloud version

      - name: Deploy to Cloud Run using Buildpacks
        uses: google-github-actions/deploy-cloudrun@v2
        with:
          service: angular-blog 
          region: europe-central2 
          source: .
</pre><div></div><div>Let''s also delete echoes.</div><pre data-language="plain">deploy:
    name: Build and Deploy
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    needs: test
</pre><div>This <strong>needs: The test </strong>will combine our deployment with tests, so if the test fails, all steps after will be canceled.</div><div></div><div>OK, it''s hanged on a fail; little fix:</div><pre data-language="plain">  - name: Run unit tests with headless Chrome
        run: npm run test -- --browsers=ChromeHeadless --watch=false --no-progress
        continue-on-error: false
</pre><div></div><div></div><div>OK, now it''s failing; now I need to fix my tests xD The fix was to delete almost all files. One test left, kek. Now seems to working</div><div class="ql-align-center"></div><div></div><div>But I think the best option is to trigger jobs on PR and then allow/block merging.</div><div></div><h2>Another action for PR check</h2><div>Let create <strong>PR_Check.yml </strong></div><pre data-language="plain">name: PR Check

on:
  pull_request:
    branches: 
      - develop 

jobs:
  test:
    name: Run Unit Tests
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout code
        uses: actions/checkout@v3

      - name: Set up Node.js
        uses: actions/setup-node@v3
        with:
          node-version: ''18''

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: npm ci

      - name: Run unit tests with headless Chrome
        run: npm run test -- --browsers=ChromeHeadless --watch=false --no-progress
</pre><div></div><div>OK, we have it, but to block merging PR, we need to migrate to the team version of GitHub.</div><div></div><div>We will add Prettier, Linter, and E2E tests, so we will correct it a little bit. Probably, we will get rid of the test when pushing to the branch, so after the PR is merged. We will move all tests to the PR side.</div><div></div><div>PS. Last, check if SSR/SSG is working. Maybe you spotted that</div><pre data-language="plain">      - name: Deploy to Cloud Run using Buildpacks
        uses: google-github-actions/deploy-cloudrun@v2
        with:
          service: angular-blog 
          region: europe-central2
          source: .
</pre><div>We only used Buildpacks, with no specified path or where to find what, and it already knows that the build contains an .mjs file for serving static content; that''s amazing!</div><div>Little proof:</div><div></div><div class="ql-align-center"></div><div></div><div>Cheers for reading!</div>', false, '2025-04-06T18:35:41+00:00', 'https://aqdbdmepncxxuanlymwr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/covers/ChatGPT%20Image%2011%20sie%202026,%2020_53_29.png', '[]'::jsonb),
  ('14452f6d-247c-4be6-858e-9412da9a7a5a', 'a3b3cf93-6956-41c7-bec0-13f95dd42f26', 'Firebase vs. Supabase vs. Appwrite: A Chill BaaS Showdown for Your Next Project', 'Choosing the right BaaS can make or break your dev workflow—Firebase excels in simplicity and real-time features, Supabase dominates with SQL power and flexibility, while Appwrite offers full control and language diversity. For quick MVPs or mobile-first apps, Firebase is a solid bet; but if you need relational data or plan to scale, Supabase is the smarter long-term play. Appwrite is the dark horse—perfect for self-hosters and teams wanting full-stack customization without vendor lock-in.', '<div>Building an app’s backend <strong>sucks</strong> – especially when you’re just trying to whip up an MVP or a pet project over a weekend. Who wants to spend nights wrestling with servers and databases (ugh) when you could be shipping features? 🙃 Enter <strong>Backend-as-a-Service (BaaS)</strong>: platforms that handle the heavy lifting (database, auth, storage, etc.) so you can focus on the fun parts (coding your app) without reinventing the wheel each time.</div><div>In this post, we’ll <strong>casually</strong> compare three popular BaaS options – <strong>Firebase</strong>, <strong>Supabase</strong>, and <strong>Appwrite</strong> – from the perspective of a fellow developer (and Angular enthusiast) who’s been down this road. We’ll look at what they offer, where they shine, and where they might make you pull your hair out. By the end, you should have a clearer idea which of these fits your small app, MVP, or side project (and when you might consider some alternatives like PocketBase, Nhost, or Back4App). Grab a beverage, and let’s dive in! 😎</div><h2>Meet the BaaS Contenders</h2><div>Before we get into the nitty-gritty, let’s introduce our three backends in friendly terms:</div><div></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Firebase</strong> – The veteran here, launched by Google. It’s a fully managed platform with a gazillion features (DB, auth, cloud functions, storage, messaging, analytics, you name it). Firebase is closed-source (proprietary), but <strong>backed by Google’s infrastructure</strong> and used by millions of apps. It’s famous for its realtime database and ease of integration, especially in mobile apps. <em>Motto:</em> <em>“Build &amp; run modern, AI-powered experiences users love with Firebase…”</em> (straight from the official site​ <a href="https://firebase.google.com/#:~:text=Make%20your%20app%20%20the,with%20Firebase%20and%20generative%20AI" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">firebase.google.com</a>).</li></ul><div></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Supabase</strong> – The new hotshot that came onto the scene in 2020. Supabase is <strong>open source</strong> and proudly calls itself “the open source Firebase alternative”​ <a href="https://github.com/supabase/supabase#:~:text=Supabase%20is%20an%20open%20source,grade%20open%20source%20tools" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">github.com</a> Under the hood it gives you a dedicated <strong>Postgres SQL database</strong>, with auto-generated APIs, auth, file storage, and edge functions (serverless funcs) — basically Firebase-like features built on enterprise-grade open source tools. You can self-host it or use Supabase’s cloud. It has gained a huge following in a short time (nearly <strong>80k GitHub stars</strong> and counting!), a testament to the hype and love from devs.</li></ul><div></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Appwrite</strong> – The flexible self-hostable option. Appwrite is also <strong>open source</strong> (over <strong>47k stars</strong> ⭐) and lets you deploy your own backend server packaged as a set of microservices. It provides <strong>databases, auth, cloud functions, storage, and even a built-in messaging system</strong> under one roof​ <a href="https://github.com/appwrite/appwrite#:~:text=Appwrite%20is%20an%20end,to%20build%20secure%20apps%20faster" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">github.com</a> <a href="https://appwrite.io/products/messaging#:~:text=Messaging%20,set%20up%20within%20minutes" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">appwrite.io</a>. You can run it yourself (Docker-based) or use Appwrite Cloud. Think of Appwrite as an all-in-one backend toolbox that you control. It’s often pitched as a Firebase alternative as well, with a focus on <strong>developer experience</strong> and broad language support.</li></ul><div></div><div>All three have generous free tiers for hobby projects (Firebase’s free Spark plan, Supabase’s free plan, Appwrite Cloud’s free tier) and scale to paid plans as your app grows. More on pricing later, but the good news is you can start tinkering with any of them for $0 upfront. 👍</div><div>Alright, introductions done. Now let’s break down the <strong>feature-by-feature comparison</strong> in a chill, dev-to-dev style. We’ll cover the core stuff like database, real-time capabilities, auth, storage, and some newer buzzwords (hello, vector databases and AI). Along the way I’ll highlight each platform’s strengths (💪) and quirks (🤕). <em>Let the showdown begin!</em> 🥊</div><h2>Database – SQL or NoSQL (and Why It Matters)</h2><div>One of the biggest differences between Firebase, Supabase, and Appwrite is their <strong>database</strong> under the hood, which affects how you structure and query your data.</div><div></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Firebase – Firestore (NoSQL)</strong>: Firebase’s primary database is <strong>Cloud Firestore</strong>, a NoSQL document database. Data is stored in collections of JSON-like documents. This schema-less approach is flexible for hierarchical or varying data, but can be a <strong>pain in the ass</strong> for highly relational data (joining collections in queries is limited, so modeling complex relationships can feel awkward). Firestore does have a pretty slick web console where you can view and edit data, and you can create collections on the fly (even via the client SDK – your first write can create a collection). Data from Firestore comes back as native objects in your code, and if you use something like AngularFire, you can even get <strong>observables</strong> out-of-the-box for real-time streams. Under the hood, Firestore supports various data types (strings, numbers, booleans, timestamps, geolocation points, arrays, maps, etc.). Fun fact: it recently even added support for <strong>vector embeddings</strong> as a data type for AI use-cases​ <a href="https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/databases/firestore-launches-at-next24#:~:text=If%20you%E2%80%99re%20trying%20to%20build,such%20as%20LangChain%20and%20LlamaIndex" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">cloud.google.com</a>! One gotcha: Firestore’s <code>Timestamp</code> is a special type (not a plain JS Date – caught me off guard the first time xD). There’s also the <strong>Firebase Realtime Database</strong> (the older predecessor to Firestore) which is a different JSON tree store – also NoSQL. But most new projects use Firestore for its querying and offline support. <strong>TL;DR:</strong> Firebase gives you a <strong>NoSQL doc DB</strong> – super easy to start, scales well, but less convenient for complex relational queries.</li></ul><div></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Supabase – Postgres (SQL)</strong>: Supabase’s whole appeal is that it gives you a <strong>full SQL database</strong> (PostgreSQL) without you having to set up Postgres yourself (<em>“without the hassle, motherf</em>**er”* as one might say 😜). If you love writing SQL or just need the power of relational schemas, Supabase is a dream. You get all the features of Postgres – tables, relationships, foreign keys, joins, indexes, you name it – <strong>Full SQL!!!</strong> including advanced stuff like triggers and stored procedures if you want​. Supabase layers an auto-generated RESTful API and a real-time engine on top of Postgres: every table you create instantly becomes accessible via REST and via their client libraries (no ORMs needed for basic CRUD). And yes, you get a web UI (Supabase Studio) where you can run SQL queries directly, design your schema with an editor, and even browse data in a table view. Essentially, it’s like having pgAdmin in your browser. The Supabase JS client returns promises for queries (since it’s talking to a REST endpoint or using websockets for realtime), which is straightforward. Data types are whatever Postgres supports – a wide range, including JSON columns if you want semi-structured data, and recently support for <strong>vector embeddings</strong> through the pgvector extension (more on that shortly). If you have a background in traditional databases or you know you’ll need to do <strong>multi-table JOINs</strong> or complex filtering, Supabase’s SQL-first approach is a huge plus. <strong>TL;DR:</strong> Supabase gives you <strong>relational SQL</strong> with all the power (and complexity) that entails – great for structured data and complex queries, perhaps overkill for simple apps that don’t need SQL’s full might.</li></ul><div></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Appwrite – Documents (NoSQL on SQL)</strong>: Appwrite’s database model is a bit unique. It presents a <strong>NoSQL-like interface</strong> (collections and documents with JSON structure), but under the hood it actually uses a <strong>SQL database (MariaDB)</strong> to store everything​ <a href="https://appwrite.io/threads/1083280333354577980#:~:text=The%20user%20wanted%20to%20know,8%20Mar%2C%202023%2C%2022%3A49" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">appwrite.io</a> <a href="https://appwrite.io/docs/products/databases/collections#:~:text=Appwrite%20uses%20collections%20as%20containers,uses%20SQL%20under%20the%20hood" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">appwrite.io</a>. You interact with Appwrite DB via its REST/SDK as if it’s a document DB (like creating collections, adding documents), and Appwrite handles mapping that into tables behind the scenes. The idea is to give the ease-of-use of NoSQL for developers, while still benefiting from the reliability of a relational DB underneath. However, you as a developer don’t directly write SQL queries – you use the Appwrite query API (which supports filtering, pagination, etc.). So, think of it as a managed NoSQL service that is backed by SQL for consistency. Appwrite’s console lets you create collections and view documents, though when our team first tried it, the UI was still evolving (it’s quite decent now). Notably, Appwrite introduced <strong>relationships</strong> between collections (experimental at first) – so you can define something akin to foreign keys or refs between documents​</li></ul><div><a href="https://appwrite.io/threads/1101855453278908578#:~:text=Appwrite%20will%20support%20both%20SQL,Appwrite%20will%20treat%20your" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">appwrite.io</a>. That’s them acknowledging that devs sometimes need relational data even in a NoSQL setup (SQL sneaking in 😅). In practice, it means Appwrite can do some joins internally if you set up a relation between collections. Types-wise, Appwrite accepts typical JSON data types (string, int, boolean, etc.) but documentation on exact supported field types was a bit hard to find; assume it covers most common needs. Queries return promise-based results via the SDK. <strong>TL;DR:</strong> Appwrite stores data as <strong>document collections</strong> (NoSQL style for ease), but commits it to a SQL database under the hood for stability. Good middle-ground if you want flexibility but also some relational features – just know you won’t be hand-crafting SQL queries (the system handles it).</div><div></div><div><strong>Common ground:</strong> All three solutions allow storing pretty complex data structures, and all support client-side caching/offline (Firebase has built-in offline support; Supabase and Appwrite being self-hostable rely on the client caching if needed or just always online requests). They also all enforce some form of <strong>schema</strong> or validation: Firestore is schema-less but you can use security rules to enforce certain structure; Supabase is schema-ed (SQL schema); Appwrite collections you define the allowed structure (fields types) when you create them. So none of these is completely wild-west with data types.</div><div>In summary, the database choice boils down to <strong>NoSQL vs SQL</strong> preference:</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1">If you want <strong>plug-and-play, flexible JSON</strong> and don’t mind structuring data to fit a NoSQL style, Firebase (Firestore) is awesome.</li><li class="mb-1">If you want <strong>good old SQL</strong> (and have complex relationships or want to use raw SQL queries), Supabase is your friend (it’s literally a managed Postgres).</li><li class="mb-1">If you like the idea of <strong>open-source and self-hosting</strong> and want a NoSQL feel but with some relational backbone, Appwrite offers a nice compromise.</li></ul><div></div><div><em>(Pro tip: If you’re an Angular dev using Firebase, check out AngularFire, which makes using Firestore super easy with RxJS. For Supabase or Appwrite, you’ll use their JS SDKs – which work fine in Angular too.)</em></div><h2>Real-time Updates – All about that Live Sync</h2><div>Modern apps often need real-time updates (think chat apps, live dashboards, etc.). The good news: <strong>all three platforms support real-time data</strong> out of the box, so your app can get updates instantly without polling. 🎉</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Firebase Realtime/Firestore</strong>: Firebase’s name is basically synonymous with real-time sync. Both its Realtime Database and Firestore can push updates to clients when data changes. With Firestore, you set up a listener (<code>onSnapshot</code> in web SDK, for example) on a query or document, and Firebase SDK will keep it open (under the hood it’s using a web socket or long-poll) and <strong>stream changes</strong> to your app. This is super handy – you write minimal code to get live updates. Latency is usually very low (they have a global infrastructure with edge servers). If you’re on Angular, AngularFire’s <code>valueChanges()</code> or <code>snapshotChanges()</code> wraps this nicely into an Observable stream. It’s one of Firebase’s strongest features – real-time is baked in deeply.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Supabase Realtime</strong>: Supabase achieves realtime by listening to Postgres <strong>walrus</strong> (Write-Ahead-Log) and broadcasting DB changes via websockets. In practice, you can subscribe to a table or even specific rows using their JS library (<code>supabase.channel(...).on(''postgres_changes'', ...)</code>). It’s pretty darn cool – you get real-time notifications when someone inserts/updates data in the database, with payloads of the new record. It feels almost as seamless as Firebase’s, though since it’s new, you might hit occasional connection quirks. Still, in my experience it’s solid. Supabase’s realtime uses <strong>websockets</strong> under the hood for low-latency push. So yes, you can build your chat app or live feed with Supabase too, no sweat.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Appwrite Realtime</strong>: Appwrite also provides realtime subscriptions. You can subscribe to changes on documents or collections via the SDK and get events when something changes. Internally, Appwrite uses <strong>websocket connections</strong> as well to push these events. They even allow subscribing to specific resource IDs (like listen to changes on document X in collection Y). This means you can also get real-time updates for things like new file uploads, user status, etc., not just DB docs. It’s a full-featured realtime engine. When I tried it, it worked similarly – you open a channel via the JS client and then handle incoming events.</li></ul><div>All three solutions’ realtime features are <strong>based on websockets and pub/sub</strong> systems, so performance is quite efficient. They handle scaling that for you on their cloud services. If you self-host Appwrite or Supabase, you’d run their realtime server components as part of the stack. For most devs, just know <strong>you don’t have to manually set up Socket.io or something – it’s built-in</strong>.</div><div>So, in terms of realtime capability, <strong>it’s a tie</strong> – <strong>Firebase, Supabase, and Appwrite all provide live data updates</strong> that just work with minimal config. 🙌</div><div><em>(One slight differentiator: Firestore can combine realtime with its offline persistence, giving you a smooth experience in flaky networks – it will serve cached data then sync when reconnect. Supabase and Appwrite rely on the client being online for realtime; offline you’d just see no new events until reconnected.)</em></div><h2>Authentication and Security – Users Made Easy</h2><div>Almost every app needs user authentication. Managing login/registration, password hashing, OAuth flows… it’s a lot. Thankfully, all three platforms come with <strong>Authentication services</strong> built-in, so you don’t have to code auth from scratch (yay!).</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Firebase Auth</strong>: Firebase Authentication is <strong>very polished</strong>. It supports email/password auth, phone number auth (with SMS codes), and OAuth providers like Google, Facebook, Twitter, GitHub, Microsoft, Apple – pretty much all the big ones. Implementing auth is usually a few lines of code (or even just some config if you use their UI library). It handles sending confirmation emails, password resets, etc. using templates you can customize. Firebase Auth issues <strong>JWT tokens</strong> for logged-in users that you can use to secure your backend calls (and these tokens integrate with Firebase’s other services’ security rules). One nice thing: since Firebase is from Google, the <strong>Sign in with Google</strong> flows are extra smooth (no surprise). Security-wise, Firebase uses these tokens and also allows defining <strong>custom claims/roles</strong> if you need (though setting those requires calling admin APIs). Also, if you use Firebase, you get easy integration with Firebase’s security rules for Firestore/Storage – so you can say “only allow writes to this document if <code>request.auth.uid</code> matches some field”, etc., making it pretty secure by design. In short, Firebase Auth is battle-tested and covers all the common scenarios for user accounts.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Supabase Auth</strong>: Supabase includes an auth system that is a fork of Netlify’s GoTrue. It also supports <strong>email/password</strong> and a bunch of OAuth providers (Google, Facebook, GitHub, etc., similar lineup). It can do magic links (email login without password) and OTP for phone if you integrate an SMS gateway. Supabase issues <strong>JWTs</strong> for auth as well, which you use in their client or send with requests. In fact, the Supabase JS client handles attaching the token to requests for you once logged in. Social login setup in Supabase involves providing your third-party app credentials (e.g. Google client ID) in the dashboard. It’s not quite as one-click as Firebase’s (which sometimes just needs enabling a toggle), but it’s straightforward. Supabase Auth integrates with the Postgres database via <strong>Row Level Security (RLS)</strong> – by default, your tables can be set to only allow operations if the user’s JWT matches certain criteria (like a column value). This is powerful, basically SQL-based security rules. For an Angular dev, using Supabase Auth feels a lot like using Firebase’s: you call <code>signUp</code>, <code>signIn</code> methods from the SDK, and you get back a user object and token. Supabase also has email confirmation built-in, etc. It might lack some niche features (I don’t recall if it has multi-factor auth yet, whereas Firebase supports 2FA in some form), but for most apps it’s sufficient. <strong>All in all:</strong> Supabase Auth gives you the core auth features with JWT and social logins, nicely integrated with its database security.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Appwrite Auth</strong>: Appwrite provides a full-blown auth system as well. You can do email/password, and it supports OAuth2 providers similarly (Google, Facebook, Github, Discord, Apple, etc.). It also has its own email confirmation and recovery flows. Like the others, Appwrite issues <strong>JWT tokens</strong> for authenticated sessions. One cool aspect: Appwrite has the notion of teams and roles as first-class concepts, so you can manage user roles/permissions at a fine-grained level. All access to resources (database documents, storage files, etc.) can be controlled by <strong>permissions</strong> that include users or teams. This means out-of-the-box <strong>role-based access control</strong> if you need it (Supabase can do similar with policies; Firebase can with custom claims, but Appwrite makes it very explicit in their API). Setting up OAuth in Appwrite Cloud is fairly easy – provide creds and boom. One thing to note: since Appwrite can be self-hosted, if you run your own instance you have to set up things like OAuth app IDs yourself (no biggie, just something to remember). From the client side, using Appwrite Auth in (say) an Angular app means using their JavaScript SDK to call <code>account.createSession()</code> or <code>account.createOAuth2Session()</code>, etc., which will handle redirects and so on. It works fine. <strong>Summary:</strong> Appwrite Auth is robust and comparable to Firebase’s – plus if you ever need custom logic, you have access to the underlying data (since you run the server or use their API, you could script stuff).</li></ul><div><strong>All three</strong> auth systems are quite solid. They all provide <strong>JWT-based auth</strong>, meaning once the user logs in, you get a token that proves their identity, and you use that for subsequent requests. They each integrate with their own services for permissioning (Firebase security rules, Supabase RLS, Appwrite permissions). And they all support the standard login methods that a modern app would need (at least the popular social logins and email/password).</div><div>One difference: <strong>Firebase’s phone auth</strong> is very handy if you need users to sign up via SMS codes (it takes care of sending the SMS for you via Firebase’s provider – though watch out, on free tier phone auth is limited and on paid it costs per SMS). Supabase and Appwrite can do phone login but require you to hook up an SMS service like Twilio or a similar integration yourself; not as turnkey as Firebase’s solution.</div><div>But generally, <strong>no clear loser here</strong> – you won’t go wrong with any of them for basic auth. It might come down to whether you want a managed, out-of-the-box approach (Firebase) or open source control (Supabase/Appwrite) in the long run.</div><div><em>(Security PSA: Always configure your security rules or policies! All three providers give you ways to restrict data access. Don’t skip that or your data could be wide open. Thankfully, each platform’s defaults guide you toward safety – e.g., by default Firestore rules lock everything, Supabase requires explicit RLS to open data, Appwrite requires you to assign read/write permissions to each document or it’s not public.)</em></div><h2>Cloud Functions – Extending with Server Logic</h2><div>Sooner or later, your app will need to run some <strong>server-side logic</strong> that goes beyond basic CRUD. Maybe image processing, sending a complex transaction email, interacting with third-party APIs, or implementing custom business logic that shouldn’t run on the client for security. That’s where <strong>serverless functions</strong> (a.k.a. cloud functions) come in. All three platforms allow you to write and deploy custom backend code that runs on their infrastructure.</div><div></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Firebase Functions</strong>: Firebase integrates with <strong>Google Cloud Functions</strong>. You write functions (in Node.js runtime by default; officially they also support Python now, and you can use other languages via Cloud Functions gen2 if you know how, but the Firebase CLI mainly targets Node/TS). These functions can be HTTP endpoints or triggered by events (like a Firestore write, an auth creation, or a cron schedule via Cloud Scheduler). Firebase makes it super easy to deploy them: you just write your JS/TS code in the <code>functions</code> folder and run <code>firebase deploy</code>. Then you get a URL for each HTTP function, and you can call it from your app. These run in Google’s servers, auto-scaling as needed. For Angular devs, this is often how you’d implement any sensitive logic (like making a payment through Stripe – you’d call a Firebase Function from your Angular app to do it securely). The downside? Cold starts can be a bit slow on free tier (Google Cloud is improving this though), and debugging functions sometimes is cumbersome. But overall, it’s a well-oiled machine. <strong>Language support:</strong> Officially Node.js (JavaScript/TypeScript) and Python (in beta) via Firebase tooling​. Unofficially, GCP functions support more, but let’s keep it simple. If you needed to run other languages, you might deploy a Cloud Run container or something – but that’s outside Firebase’s immediate scope.</li></ul><div></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Supabase Edge Functions</strong>: Supabase offers <strong>Edge Functions</strong>, which are serverless functions that run on <strong>Deno</strong> (a secure JavaScript/TypeScript runtime, similar to Node but with some differences). You write your function in TS, test locally with the Supabase CLI, then deploy it. These functions are actually deployed globally (they use Deno Deploy or Cloudflare under the hood – they’re <em>edge</em> functions). That means they are very fast and low latency for users around the world 🌎. Supabase functions are invoked via HTTP just like Firebase’s. One cool thing: because they run on Deno, you can use modern ES modules and even try other languages that compile to Wasm (theoretically), but primarily you’ll use JS/TS. The Supabase CLI will bundle and deploy the code. Also, these functions have access to your Supabase project’s secrets and can connect to the database (via server admin privileges) if needed. In terms of performance, Supabase Edge Functions have <strong>fast cold starts</strong> (often sub-second) because Deno is quite optimized for that. They only officially support JS/TS at the moment​, which covers most use cases. If you absolutely need another language, you could always host your own service, but then you’re veering outside Supabase’s offerings. So, <strong>language support</strong>: JavaScript/TypeScript officially. The developer experience with these is great if you like TS – you get type safety and all.</li></ul><div></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Appwrite Cloud Functions</strong>: Appwrite takes a different approach by supporting <strong>a bunch of languages</strong> for its cloud functions (they just call them <em>Functions</em> in Appwrite). You can write your function in <strong>Node (JS/TS)</strong>, <strong>Python</strong>, <strong>Dart</strong>, <strong>PHP</strong>, <strong>Ruby</strong>, <strong>Go</strong>, and even <strong>Java or .NET</strong> (Kotlin, C# via community, etc.)​. Basically, they provide runtime environments for many popular languages. This is great because you can pick the language you’re most comfortable with or that best fits the task (e.g., use Python for a data science task, or Go for performance). To deploy a function in Appwrite, you package your code (often as a tar or via their CLI) and upload it to Appwrite. If self-hosting, it runs it in a container. If using Appwrite Cloud, they handle the infrastructure. You can trigger these functions via HTTP endpoints or set them to run on certain events (like database document changes) or even schedule them (Appwrite supports CRON schedules for functions as of recent versions). The flexibility is awesome, though the setup is a bit more involved than, say, Firebase’s simple <code>firebase deploy</code>. You might have to include a <code>package.json</code> for Node or specify runtime in Appwrite’s console. But once set up, it’s smooth. <strong>Performance:</strong> since these might run in Docker containers, cold start could be a bit heavier depending on language (spinning up a Python runtime might be a tad slower than a lightweight JS on edge). But for moderate use, it’s fine. <strong>In summary:</strong> Appwrite Functions shine in language flexibility. If you have non-JS code to run, Appwrite is a winner here.</li></ul><div></div><div>All three platforms acknowledge that you <em>shouldn’t put secret logic on the client</em>, and they provide these function capabilities so you can do the sensitive stuff securely on the backend.</div><div>One more thing: <strong>environment integration</strong>. Firebase functions can easily call other Firebase services with admin privileges (since they run in Google Cloud with service account). Supabase functions run with a service key so they can bypass RLS or do privileged DB ops if needed. Appwrite functions run with API keys/privileges you set, so they can also interact with the Appwrite API (or any external API). So you can always perform operations on your database or storage from within these functions beyond what a client could do.</div><div><strong>Scheduling</strong>: Firebase doesn’t have built-in cron in Firebase itself, but you can use Google Cloud Scheduler or their pubsub triggers (there’s a way to trigger functions on schedule via an extension). Supabase doesn’t (at the moment) have a native scheduler in UI, but you can use PG cron extension or GitHub Actions or another external trigger. Appwrite, if I recall, added a simple schedule feature for functions (so you can run something every X minutes/hour). If not, you’d handle externally. Minor detail unless you need cron jobs.</div><div></div><div>To sum up the vibe:</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1">If you’re fine with <strong>JS/TS</strong>, Firebase and Supabase have very streamlined function deployment.</li><li class="mb-1">If you want to write backend code in <strong>other languages</strong>, Appwrite is your friend (no need to learn Node if you want to use Go or Python).</li><li class="mb-1">Performance-wise, Supabase’s edge functions might edge out (pun intended) for global low-latency needs, but for most apps, differences are negligible.</li></ul><h2>File Storage and Assets – Uploading Images and Stuff</h2><div>Storing user-uploaded files (images, PDFs, etc.) is another common backend need. Here’s how each platform tackles it:</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Firebase Storage</strong>: Firebase offers <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> for files, which is basically an integration of Google Cloud Storage (GCS) behind the scenes. It’s a scalable object storage (think of it like Amazon S3 but on Google). It’s great for storing images, videos, any blobs. You use Firebase SDK to upload/download files. By default files are private, and you use security rules to control access (e.g., only allow download if user is auth, or only allow write to <code>/avatars/{uid}</code> if <code>request.auth.uid == uid</code>). Firebase Storage doesn’t automatically serve through a CDN, <em>but</em> since it’s on Google’s network, it’s pretty fast globally. You can also easily combine it with Firebase Hosting or Cloud CDN for caching if needed. One thing: Firebase Storage doesn’t have built-in image transformations. If you need thumbnails or resizing, you’d typically use a <strong>Cloud Function</strong> or a Firebase Extension (they have an extension that generates thumbnails on upload). It’s not on-the-fly, but you can set up those pipelines. It does have the ability to serve files via download URLs or even token-based public URLs. For most small apps, it’s straightforward: you can store user profile pics, etc., and not worry about capacity (free tier is quite generous, beyond that pay per GB).</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Supabase Storage</strong>: Supabase has a <strong>Storage module</strong> which gives you an S3-like bucket system. Under the hood, Supabase’s managed service uses cloud object storage (I think on AWS or Google) and fronted by a global CDN. Yes, Supabase claims files are served via a <strong>CDN by default</strong>​, which means faster downloads worldwide and also less load on your origin. You create “buckets” and can set them public or private. If public, files get a URL that can be accessed directly (via the CDN). If private, you’ll need to use Supabase’s API (with auth) to retrieve. You can upload via their SDK or directly via HTTP PUT. Supabase’s storage is tightly integrated with the auth system – meaning you can enforce that only certain users can access certain buckets or files, etc., through policies. One neat feature: they’ve been working on an <strong>Image Transformation API</strong> (in beta)​. This would let you request a file with query params like <code>?width=300&amp;height=300</code> to get a resized version, for example. It’s still beta, but coming. For now, you might have to implement any heavy file processing via an Edge Function. Overall, Supabase Storage covers the basics well and the CDN out-of-the-box is a sweet advantage (your images and videos will load quickly for users everywhere without you doing anything extra).</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Appwrite Storage</strong>: Appwrite includes a <strong>Storage service</strong> for files. If you self-host, by default it stores files on the local filesystem (in the Docker volume). In cloud, they likely use an object storage behind the scenes. You organize files by “buckets” as well, similar concept to Supabase. Appwrite’s kicker: it provides <strong>built-in image transformation</strong> APIs. You can request images with resizing, cropping, quality adjustments just by adding query parameters to the file URL (very handy for generating thumbnails or different resolutions on the fly)​. This feature is available out-of-the-box (no need to write a function for it). Appwrite doesn’t automatically put a CDN in front (unless their Cloud does under the hood), so if global distribution is a concern you might need to integrate a CDN yourself for self-hosted. But for small apps it’s usually fine. Access control: Appwrite uses its same permissions system for files. When you upload a file, you can assign which users (or roles) can read or write it. So you can make files public or keep them private to certain users easily. The SDK makes file upload simple (you can even upload via an HTML form file object). <strong>In short:</strong> Appwrite’s storage does what you need, and the <strong>on-the-fly image manipulation</strong> is a standout feature (Supabase is catching up on that, Firebase needs an extension or custom code for it).</li></ul><div></div><div>To decide:</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1">If you need <strong>global CDN</strong> out of the box and plan to serve a lot of public content, Supabase might be optimal.</li><li class="mb-1">If you love the idea of <strong>easy image resizing on the fly</strong>, Appwrite’s got it built-in (Supabase likely soon, Firebase via extension).</li><li class="mb-1">Firebase’s storage is super reliable and integrates with Google’s ecosystem, but may need a bit more work to set up things like CDN or image processing pipelines (still, it’s tried-and-true for many apps).</li></ul><div>Storage pricing and limits are different for each, but in general all have free quotas (Firebase’s free 5GB, Supabase free some GB, Appwrite Cloud free tier includes some storage too). For a small app, you likely won’t hit limits.</div><div><em>One more difference: Appwrite and Supabase storage API can return file listings and such easily via SDK. Firebase’s API is a bit less straightforward (you don’t list files via client SDK without using Firebase Admin or some workaround, which is a known annoyance; you often have to store file metadata in Firestore if you want to list them). Keep that in mind if you need to show galleries of uploaded files – with Firebase, plan to track uploads in the database manually.</em></div><h2>Going AI: Vector Databases and ML Goodies</h2><div>Since AI features are all the rage (chatbots, semantic search, recommendations, etc.), it’s worth noting how these platforms support <strong>vector data</strong> and AI integration:</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Firebase (Firestore + AI integration)</strong>: Google has been baking AI features into Firebase recently. As of 2024, <strong>Firestore supports vector search natively</strong>​</li></ul><div><a href="https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/databases/firestore-launches-at-next24#:~:text=If%20you%E2%80%99re%20trying%20to%20build,such%20as%20LangChain%20and%20LlamaIndex" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">cloud.google.com</a>. This means you can store high-dimensional vectors (embeddings) as a field and run similarity queries (find nearest vectors) right in Firestore. They even launched a Firebase <strong>GenAI Kit (Genkit)</strong> to help integrate with large language models and orchestrate AI workflows​ <a href="https://firebase.blog/posts/2024/05/whats-new-at-google-io/#:~:text=What%27s%20new%20in%20Firebase%20at,models%2C%20vector%20stores%2C%20evaluators%2C" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">firebase.blog</a> . And of course, being on Google Cloud, Firebase can tap into things like Vertex AI. They have extensions and sample apps demonstrating chatbots that use Firestore as a knowledge base with vector embeddings. In simpler terms, Firebase now lets you do some of what specialized vector DBs (like Pinecone) do, albeit at smaller scale (good enough for many apps). Additionally, Firebase offers ML Kit for on-device ML, and other AI-driven services (like content moderation APIs via extensions). So if you want to <strong>add AI features quickly</strong>, Firebase has an edge with its integrated offerings. It’s not just a backend, it’s kind of an entire app development platform including AI assistance.</div><div></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Supabase (pgvector and embeddings)</strong>: Supabase, leveraging Postgres, has jumped on the vector bandwagon too. Postgres can be extended with the <strong>pgvector</strong> extension, and Supabase makes it easy to enable. With it, you can add a column of type <code>VECTOR</code> to your table to store embedding arrays, and query for nearest neighbors (cosine similarity or Euclidean distance) efficiently. Supabase even wrote about using vectors for AI apps (embedding text and storing vectors for similarity search). So you can absolutely build an AI-powered feature (like semantic text search or recommendations) with Supabase. You’d generate embeddings using your model or an API (like OpenAI) on the app side or in a function, store them in the DB, and query as needed. It’s not as plug-and-play as Firebase’s out-of-the-box integration (since Firebase can automatically generate embeddings via an extension​ <a href="https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/databases/firestore-launches-at-next24#:~:text=recommendations%20engine%2C%20Firestore%20has%20you,popular%20generative%20AI%20libraries%20such" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">cloud.google.com</a>), but it’s as powerful as you want it to be – you have full SQL control. Also, Supabase has some tutorials integrating with <strong>LangChain</strong> etc., and you can always call out to OpenAI API from a Supabase Edge Function if needed. So, Supabase is definitely <strong>AI-friendly</strong>, just a bit more DIY in connecting the parts compared to Firebase’s one-stop shop.</li></ul><div></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Appwrite (integration with external Vector DBs)</strong>: Appwrite itself doesn’t have a native vector search feature within its core database as of now (its DB is built on MariaDB, which isn’t optimized for vector similarity). However, Appwrite is flexible: you can integrate external databases or services. In fact, the Appwrite team put out examples of integrating <strong>Pinecone</strong> or <strong>Upstash Vector</strong> as external vector databases​ <a href="https://appwrite.io/docs/products/ai/integrations/pinecone#:~:text=Integrating%20Pinecone%20,building%20recommendation%20systems%2C%20search" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">appwrite.io</a> ​<a href="https://appwrite.io/blog/post/integrate-sql-nosql-vector-graph-or-any-database-into-your-appwrite-project#:~:text=Integrate%20SQL%2C%20NoSQL%2C%20Vector%2C%20Graph%2C,for%20any%20input%20you%20provide" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">appwrite.io</a>. You could use Appwrite Cloud Functions to communicate with those services. Also, Appwrite announced some <strong>AI integrations</strong> for things like using OpenAI or other ML services in your Appwrite functions (not built-in ML, but easier integration points). So while Appwrite might not have a checkbox “enable vector search” in its console, you’re not barred from doing AI stuff – you’ll just plug another tool in. And because Appwrite is self-hostable, you could even run a small vector DB alongside it in your own infra if you wanted. As for other AI features: Appwrite doesn’t bundle an ML kit or anything, but you can use any library in a function. For example, you could run sentiment analysis in a Python function or use TensorFlow.js in a Node function. It’s all up to you.</li></ul><div></div><div>In summary: <strong>Firebase</strong> is ahead in terms of built-in AI/ML support (makes sense given Google’s focus there), <strong>Supabase</strong> gives you the building blocks (SQL + vectors) to roll your own easily, and <strong>Appwrite</strong> lets you integrate what you need but doesn’t have native AI features yet. For many small apps, this may not matter, but if you plan to include, say, a smart search or recommendation based on embeddings, know that both Firebase and Supabase can handle that within their system.</div><div>If you’re a newbie to AI but want to play around, Firebase’s managed approach might let you experiment faster (they have a bunch of tutorials on this). If you’re comfortable with writing some code to glue things, Supabase and Appwrite are fine too.</div><h2>Messaging and Notifications – Email, SMS, and Push</h2><div>How do you send an email verification, or a password reset, or a push notification to your mobile app? BaaS platforms often provide some way to send messages/notifications to users:</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Firebase Messaging and Extensions</strong>: Firebase offers <strong>Cloud Messaging (FCM)</strong> for push notifications. That covers push to iOS/Android (and web push) very well – it’s the go-to service for mobile push. It’s free and unlimited. For emails/SMS, Firebase Auth already handles the basic emails (verification, password reset) for you via templates. For custom emails or SMS, Firebase itself doesn’t have a built-in service, but you can use third-party via Cloud Functions or use Firebase Extensions. For example, there’s an extension to send email via SendGrid, or you could call Twilio from a function for SMS. There’s also <strong>Firebase In-App Messaging</strong> (a different product for nudging users inside the app) and <strong>Firebase Extensions</strong> for things like phone SMS (they have an extension to send an SMS via Twilio for phone auth beyond the free quota, etc.). So, while not unified under one API, Firebase’s ecosystem definitely covers notifications: FCM for push, and partners for email/SMS.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Supabase</strong>: Supabase doesn’t natively have a “send email or SMS” service in the same sense. However, Supabase Auth will send confirmation emails by default (they use a built-in mailer service with a Supabase-branded sender unless you configure SMTP). For anything else, you’d typically use an <strong>Edge Function</strong> to call an email API (like SendGrid, Mailgun) or an SMS API. They have guides for integrating OneSignal for push notifications​ <a href="https://supabase.com/partners/onesignal#:~:text=OneSignal%20,Push%20notifications%3B%20SMS%3B%20Emails" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">supabase.com</a> (OneSignal is a third-party that handles push cross-platform). Essentially, Supabase believes in using best-of-breed external services for messaging rather than re-inventing them. So you get the hooks (like you can trigger a function on row insert to send a notification). There’s not an out-of-the-box UI for “send this email to user X” in Supabase. That said, the building blocks are all there (and because you have a full DB, you can even set up some trigger functions in SQL to call webhooks for notifications, if you’re an SQL wizard). It’s a bit more manual but totally doable. For push, likely you’ll integrate with FCM or OneSignal anyway for the device handling.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Appwrite Messaging</strong>: Appwrite actually introduced a <strong>Messaging API</strong> that unifies sending <strong>emails, SMS, and push notifications</strong> from your Appwrite backend​ <a href="https://appwrite.io/products/messaging#:~:text=Messaging%20,set%20up%20within%20minutes" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">appwrite.io</a>. This is pretty sweet – via Appwrite’s SDK or console, you can send an email to a user, or an SMS, or a push notification, all via one service. Under the hood, you configure providers (for email it might use an SMTP server you configure, for SMS you’d configure Twilio or a similar SMS gateway, for push you’d set up your FCM/APNs keys). Once set, Appwrite’s API handles sending. It also logs the messages and their status in the Appwrite console​ <a href="https://appwrite.io/docs/products/messaging/messages#:~:text=Each%20time%20you%20send%20or,displayed%20in%20the%20Messages%20tab" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">appwrite.io</a>. This is a fairly new feature, but it means Appwrite is aiming to be a one-stop backend that also handles your communications. Instead of writing a cloud function to send a welcome email, you might call Appwrite’s messaging API directly with the email template and user ID. This can speed up development if you’re not familiar with third-party email services.</li></ul><div></div><div>So in terms of <strong>out-of-the-box messaging</strong>:</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Appwrite</strong> has a unified built-in messaging system for push/email/SMS (you’ll still need to plug in credentials for SMS gateway or email SMTP, but the code integration is unified).</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Firebase</strong> provides native push (FCM) and built-in auth emails, but for other use-cases you’d use extensions or external services – slightly more setup, but many community solutions exist.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Supabase</strong> leaves it to you to integrate external services via functions; no one-click solution in the dashboard (at least as of now).</li></ul><div>For many simple apps, default auth emails are enough and maybe you don’t need to send any other emails. If you do, consider if you want to be <strong>in control of messaging</strong> (Appwrite might appeal) or you’re okay wiring up an external service (Supabase, Firebase approach).</div><div>Push notifications: If you’re building a mobile app, Firebase Cloud Messaging is the standard, and guess what, you can actually use FCM <strong>with any backend</strong> – you just need the device tokens and to call FCM API. So even if you use Supabase or Appwrite, you might still use FCM for push (Appwrite likely uses FCM under the hood for push anyway). So push is essentially a non-issue – all roads lead to FCM or APNs at some point for mobile.</div><h2>Dev Experience and Community</h2><div>Let’s talk about the developer experience, community, and other “ecosystem” goodies that come with each platform, because this can hugely impact your happiness as a dev:</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Firebase DX &amp; Ecosystem</strong>: Firebase has been around for a while and has <strong>excellent documentation and tutorials</strong>. The SDKs for web (JS), Android, iOS, and even admin (Node, Java, Python) are mature. If you’re using Angular, the AngularFire library maintained by the community makes integration idiomatic. Firebase also has a ton of extra products that tie in: Crashlytics for crash reports, Analytics, Remote Config, Performance Monitoring, etc. This can be amazing for a holistic app solution – you’re not just getting a database, you’re getting a full suite (this is the “lot of addons” the slide mentions: analytics, A/B testing, app distribution for beta apps, dynamic links, etc.​). The downside is these are all separate things to learn and set up, but they work nicely together. Community-wise, Firebase has a massive user base, meaning lots of StackOverflow answers, YouTube tutorials, and community projects. It’s not open source, but the usage is ubiquitous. You’ll find Firebase help more easily than Supabase/Appwrite simply because of its age and reach. One DX pain: some Firebase stuff (like security rules) can be tricky to debug, and some features are clearly geared to Google Cloud (occasionally you have to step into GCP console, which can be confusing for newcomers who just know Firebase). But overall, <strong>Firebase is very beginner-friendly</strong> to start with (maybe less so to master fully, but to get going it’s great).</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Supabase DX &amp; Community</strong>: Supabase being open source has attracted many developers. Its docs are generally good and improving. They often tout that “if you know Firebase, here’s how we do it differently.” One huge plus: everything is <strong>transparent</strong> – you can inspect their GitHub, you can even self-host to try features or debug. The community is vibrant (lots of contributions, a Discord server, etc.). Supabase’s rapid growth (from 0 to ~80k stars in a few years) shows devs are excited. That also means lots of third-party content emerging: blogs, example projects, integrations (for example, there’s supabase + Next.js starter kits, etc.). For Angular specifically, there’s not a dedicated Angular-Supabase library (you just use the JS SDK), but that’s fine. The company behind Supabase is active in shipping new features almost monthly (recently: vector support, Deno functions, expanded auth providers, etc.). This fast pace is exciting but can mean occasional breaking changes or things to catch up on. Supabase’s dashboard is quite nice – crafting tables and policies is mostly point-and-click with SQL for advanced cases. And because it’s Postgres, you can connect any Postgres client to the DB for advanced management (like connecting TablePlus or any DB GUI if you want). In terms of support, the community helps a lot, and there’s paid support for enterprise users. The <strong>DX</strong> is generally aimed at developers who are comfortable with code (SQL, etc.). I’d say Supabase is <strong>almost as easy as Firebase for simple stuff</strong> and can be easier for complex stuff if you’re an SQL nerd. The learning curve might be slightly higher for newbies who have never seen SQL or REST APIs, but many devs pick it up quickly.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Appwrite DX &amp; Community</strong>: Appwrite also has a growing community (those 47k stars didn’t come from nowhere!). It’s a bit more low-level in some ways, because you might self-host and need to use Docker, etc., which for a newbie could be daunting. However, Appwrite Cloud removes that friction by hosting it for you. The docs cover a lot, and they have SDKs for many languages, which is great if you’re not a JavaScript dev (though for our mostly Angular crowd, JS/TS SDK is what you’ll use, which is solid). Appwrite’s design is very developer-centric: the API is consistent, and everything (db, storage, etc.) is accessible via the same kind of REST calls or SDK methods. The learning curve might come from understanding its concepts like collections and permissions, but once you do, it’s powerful. The community is enthusiastic, and because it’s open source, you can open issues, contribute, etc. Appwrite also has a <strong>Discord</strong> and community support. It might not be as famous in some circles as Supabase, but it’s certainly not obscure. One thing to consider is that Appwrite being relatively young means fewer big reference apps or tutorials compared to Firebase. But it’s catching up; plus many concepts from Firebase (like auth, storage) carry over, just implemented differently. If you like understanding how things work, Appwrite is nice because nothing is hidden behind the curtain – it’s all open. And if something doesn’t suit you, you could even fork it (it’s open source after all) – though that’s extreme; probably you won’t need to.</li></ul><div><strong>Community “hype” check (just for fun):</strong> On GitHub, Supabase and Appwrite have a lot of stars (hype-driven dev, anyone?). Firebase, not being a single repo, has no star count, but trust me it’s used by countless apps. So popularity-wise:</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1">Firebase: very widely used in production (backed by Google, so many companies trust it).</li><li class="mb-1">Supabase: trending among web devs, especially those who like open source.</li><li class="mb-1">Appwrite: loved by those who prefer self-hostable solutions and by the Flutter community (Appwrite has strong Flutter support, for example).</li></ul><div><strong>Debugging &amp; Tooling:</strong> Firebase has an emulator suite to run things locally (Firestore, functions, etc.) which is awesome for development. Supabase has a local Docker stack you can run for dev (or use their cloud dev environment) – also great if you want to try things offline. Appwrite, being self-hostable, can run locally via Docker too for dev/testing. So all allow some local dev story.</div><div>Overall, in terms of developer <em>happiness</em>: If you want <strong>polish and lots of integrated tools</strong>, Firebase might feel nice; if you want <strong>freedom and SQL power</strong>, Supabase feels empowering; if you want <strong>control and flexibility in languages and hosting</strong>, Appwrite feels like a dev’s playground.</div><h2>Pros and Cons Summary</h2><div>Let’s break down the <strong>strengths and drawbacks</strong> of each option in a quick list format for easy scanning:</div><div><strong>Firebase – Pros:</strong></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Mature &amp; Feature-Rich:</strong> Tons of services (DB, auth, hosting, ML, analytics, etc.) in one platform – you can build entire apps with it alone.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Realtime and Offline:</strong> Real-time sync is best-in-class, and offline support for Firestore is built in (great for mobile apps).</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Easy Auth &amp; Integrations:</strong> Setting up auth providers is very straightforward; also has many extensions to add functionality (search, payments, etc.) with minimal code.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Scalable &amp; Managed:</strong> Backed by Google’s infra – it will scale automatically, and you don’t worry about servers or updates. Blaze plan is pay-as-you-go with fine-grained pricing (no hard limits, you pay for what you use, and it’s free below certain thresholds) – no surprise monthly bills unless you massively exceed free usage.</li></ul><div><strong>Firebase – Cons:</strong></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Proprietary Lock-in:</strong> It’s not open source. You can’t self-host. You’re kinda locked into Google’s ecosystem (moving off Firebase later can be a <strong>pain</strong>).</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>NoSQL Only:</strong> Firestore’s data model can be limiting for complex relational queries. You might end up doing client-side joins or duplicating data. <strong>“Dealing with relations with Firebase is pain in the ass.”</strong> – as one dev famously said.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Cloud Functions Limits:</strong> Functions default to Node environment – other languages need workarounds. Cold starts and debugging can be frustrating. Also, testing security rules and functions requires using their emulator, which has its own learning curve.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Pricing Surprises:</strong> While no monthly fee, certain things (like database reads/writes, outbound networking from functions) can add up if you’re not careful. There have been horror stories of devs accidentally incurring big bills by misconfiguring rules or leaving a loop writing to the db. Budget alerts and limits exist but one must configure them.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Bugs/Issues:</strong> Despite being mature, Firebase isn’t perfect. Developers have run into occasional SDK bugs, and the “supported by Google, but have a lot bug” comment likely refers to things like annoying issues in Firebase console or quirks in Firestore indexing. Usually not deal-breakers, but worth noting.</li></ul><div><strong>Supabase – Pros:</strong></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>SQL Power:</strong> Full Postgres under the hood – you can do efficient joins, use foreign keys, run any SQL query. Great for relational data and complex logic in the DB (e.g., you can use SQL functions, triggers).</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Open Source &amp; Self-Host:</strong> No black boxes – you can inspect the code, run it yourself, even tweak it. This avoids lock-in and gives you flexibility (e.g., migrate from Supabase Cloud to your own Postgres server if needed).</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Auto API &amp; Lovely DX:</strong> Instant REST and real-time API on your data, plus a slick web UI for management. It feels magical to have your schema changes immediately reflected in APIs and docs​. And TypeScript support in the client is excellent (they even generate types based on your schema).</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Edge Functions (Fast):</strong> Functions deployed globally for low latency. And you develop in TypeScript, which many web devs love. They’re quite speedy compared to some traditional cloud functions.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Community &amp; Integrations:</strong> Supabase integrates well with tools like Vercel (one-click integration to sync env vars, etc.)​. Many third-party libs (e.g., for Auth UI) support Supabase. Active community means quick help and rapid feature development.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Rich Features:</strong> Everything Firebase has, Supabase is trying to match or exceed: auth (with policies), storage (with CDN), realtime, and even vector search. Plus features like <strong>Postgres extensions</strong> give you extra capabilities (full-text search, geospatial, etc. easily enabled). And yeah, <strong>full text search</strong> is native in Postgres – a win over Firestore’s limited querying.</li></ul><div><strong>Supabase – Cons:</strong></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Still Maturing:</strong> It’s newer, so not as battle-tested at massive scale as Firebase. There might be sharp edges or less guidance on unusual use cases. (Though plenty of big apps do use it nowadays.)</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>SQL Overhead:</strong> If you’re not used to designing schemas or writing SQL, there’s a learning curve. Writing security policies in SQL (RLS) can also be tricky to get right (though arguably no trickier than Firebase rules, just different).</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Hosted Limitations:</strong> The Supabase Cloud free tier can auto-pause your project after periods of inactivity (to save resources)​ <a href="https://www.restack.io/docs/supabase-knowledge-supabase-delete-project-guide#:~:text=Supabase%20Project%20Deletion%20Guide%20,For%20those" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">restack.io</a>– which can be annoying if you come back to it and find it “cold”. Also the free DB might have limited performance (it’s a shared instance). The next tier (Pro) is $25/month, which is reasonable but a jump from $0. There’s currently no “pay as you go” minute-by-minute scaling; it’s either free or $25+ monthly.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>No Multi-Region DB (yet):</strong> Firebase replicates data globally behind the scenes (for Firestore). Supabase’s Postgres lives in one region (unless you do complex multi-region setups yourself). This usually doesn’t matter for small apps, but global scale multi-region writes can be challenging on a single Postgres. They do offer read replicas and such for higher plans, but it’s not as seamless as Google’s magic.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Mobile SDKs catching up:</strong> Supabase’s primary client is JS (and a Dart one for Flutter). For native iOS/Android, you might use the REST API or community SDKs. Firebase has first-party SDKs for those. Not a big deal if you’re mostly web/Angular, but worth noting if you plan to go cross-platform.</li></ul><div><strong>Appwrite – Pros:</strong></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Self-Host Freedom:</strong> You can run Appwrite anywhere – your local machine, a VPS, Docker on your NAS, etc. Data stays in your control. Great for privacy or on-prem requirements. And if you use Appwrite Cloud, you can later migrate to self-host (or vice versa) relatively easily since it’s the same software running​.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Many SDKs/Languages:</strong> Appwrite is not just JS-centric. It has official SDKs for Python, Dart (Flutter), Ruby, Go, PHP, etc. If tomorrow you decide to build a Swift UI iOS app or a Flutter app in addition to your Angular web app, Appwrite has you covered with native SDKs. You’re not stuck with just REST calls or community libs.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Granular Control:</strong> The permission model is very granular and baked in. You can, for instance, share a particular document with a specific user or team easily. This fine-grained ACL approach can simplify complex sharing use cases.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Functions in Any Language:</strong> We mentioned it, but it’s worth highlighting as a pro: you are not forced into Node/TS for cloud functions. If your team has a Python expert for example, they can write an Appwrite function in Python. This lowers the barrier to adopting serverless for teams with diverse language skills.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Active Community &amp; Improvements:</strong> Appwrite’s community is passionate. They even have a <strong>$50 free credit</strong> for new users as an incentive to try the cloud​. Features have been expanding (the recent addition of messaging, the introduction of relationships in DB, etc.). It’s under heavy development by an active core team, meaning bugs get fixed and new features (like future support for alternative DB adapters) are on the horizon.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Forkable &amp; Customizable:</strong> If you need a custom feature, you can fork the Appwrite repo, add it, or contribute upstream. For example, if you wanted a new auth provider supported, you could implement it. This is the power of open source that a closed platform can’t offer.</li></ul><div><strong>Appwrite – Cons:</strong></div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>Resource Intensive:</strong> Running Appwrite (especially self-hosted) means running multiple Docker services (database, storage, functions runtime, etc.). For a tiny hobby project, that’s heavier than something like pocketbase (which is just one small binary). If you use their cloud, they handle it, but self-hosters need to ensure their server can handle the load.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Young Platform Quirks:</strong> As a younger platform, there may be missing features or rough edges. e.g., the DB is not as query-flexible as Firestore or Postgres (no complex joins except what their relations feature provides, and no arbitrary SQL queries). You might occasionally find “can Appwrite do X?” and the answer is “not yet, but you can work around it or it’s planned”.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Less Big-scale Proof:</strong> Fewer known large-scale deployments publicly. It’s likely fine for most app sizes in this context (small apps, MVPs), but if you became the next Twitter, you’d have to ensure Appwrite scales or be ready to shard things. (Granted, scaling to millions on any platform including Firebase has its own challenges!)</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Docs/Support Slightly Less Extensive:</strong> While docs are decent, they may not cover as many edge cases, and community answers are fewer just because the user base is smaller than Firebase’s. So you might have to rely more on official Discord or GitHub issues for support.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Migrating from others not trivial:</strong> If you start on Firebase and want to move to Appwrite, it’s not drop-in (different data model, etc.). That’s true for any migration though. But since Appwrite isn’t “standard” tech under the hood (it’s its own thing on top of MariaDB), you can’t just point an existing database at it. You’d typically start a project on Appwrite fresh.</li></ul><div>Whew! That’s a lot, but hopefully it surfaces the key points about each platform. 😅</div><h2>So, Which One Should I Use?</h2><div><strong>The million-dollar question.</strong> It really depends on your project’s needs and your priorities. Here’s some straight talk recommendations:</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>For a small app or MVP with minimal data and simple auth</strong> (like you just need users to sign in and do basic CRUD): <strong>Firebase</strong> is a solid choice. It will let you move fast with its easy SDKs and managed services. Especially if you’re new to backend stuff, Firebase is very newbie-friendly – you can get sign-in, database, and hosting working without much DevOps knowledge. The free tier will likely cover you, and you won’t have to worry about scaling or maintenance at all. Example: a simple Angular app that needs to save user profiles and maybe some content – Firebase will have you covered in no time.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>For a more complex app or if you anticipate growth and need SQL</strong>: <strong>Supabase</strong> shines here. If your app might grow into something bigger, or you just prefer relational DB for its flexibility (maybe you foresee analytics queries, or linking many tables of data), starting with Supabase could save you headaches later. Also, if you might eventually need to <strong>move to your own servers or do custom backend stuff</strong>, Supabase’s open approach is nice (you could gradually migrate pieces to a custom Postgres or integrate with other tools). Example: you’re building a SaaS app with lots of interconnected data (projects, users, teams, invoices, etc.) – Supabase will handle that model well, and you can use SQL to generate reports or do joins easily. You can also pair Supabase with other services: for instance, use <strong>Supabase for the core DB/Auth</strong> and maybe use <strong>Firebase Hosting</strong> for your Angular app front-end (some people do that, since Firebase Hosting is really convenient for static site deployment). That combo was even suggested: Supabase + Firebase Hosting for bigger apps​. Supabase for data, Firebase for front-end delivery and maybe some of its add-ons.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>If you want full control or have specific requirements</strong> (self-hosting, using certain languages, etc.): <strong>Appwrite</strong> is your go-to. For example, if your project is something you need to run on-prem (say an enterprise internal tool) or you just don’t want any vendor lock and love the idea of tinkering, Appwrite is fantastic. It’s also great if your app involves multiple platforms (web + Flutter mobile, for instance) because Appwrite supports both equally well. And for the dev who says “I want to use this obscure language for my backend logic” – Appwrite’s your friend. Example: you’re building a tool for your company that must run on your own servers due to policy – Appwrite gives you Firebase-like capabilities on your infrastructure. Or you just enjoy DevOps and want to see everything under the hood (nothing wrong with that 😁). Appwrite will let you scale down to the metal if needed.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>If you’re unsure or just prototyping:</strong> You can’t really go wrong starting with either Firebase or Supabase. Both are easy to prototype on. If you hit a limitation in one, you can switch (though that involves rework, so try to choose based on what you think you’ll need). For instance, many people start on Firebase for speed, but if they later need SQL queries that Firebase can’t do, they might add Supabase or migrate to it. Conversely, if someone starts on Supabase but finds it too complex for their simple use-case, they might simplify to Firebase. Since our audience is Angular devs: Angular has first-class support for Firebase (via AngularFire). Supabase and Appwrite don’t have that Angular-specific lib, but they both work fine with plain JS/TS, so it’s not a big deal.</li></ul><div></div><div>It’s also worth noting you can <strong>mix and match</strong> services. These aren’t mutually exclusive. Some folks use Firebase Auth with a custom backend, or use Firestore alongside a SQL database, etc. In our context though, we’re focusing on using one BaaS to handle most of the needs.</div><div></div><div><strong>Final thought:</strong> <em>Use the right tool for the job.</em> For a quick hobby app, maybe don’t over-engineer – Firebase could be the quickest to show results. For a project you plan to maintain long-term and maybe scale, consider the flexibility of Supabase or Appwrite. And remember, none of these are a silver bullet; you still need to write solid code and architecture. But they sure as hell can save you from writing boilerplate backend code and managing infrastructure, which is a big win 🎉.</div><h2>Other BaaS Alternatives to Consider</h2><div>While Firebase, Supabase, and Appwrite are the talk of the town, there are other Backend-as-a-Service options out there that might tickle your fancy:</div><ul class="list-disc ml-5"><li class="mb-1"><strong>PocketBase</strong> – An interesting one: it’s an <strong>open source backend in one tiny Go binary</strong>. It includes an embedded SQLite database (so it’s serverless in the sense of not needing a separate DB server), plus auth, file storage, and real-time subscriptions. It’s super lightweight – you can literally run it on a Raspberry Pi or embed it in an Electron app. It has an admin UI built-in. PocketBase has gained ~45k stars on GitHub, so it''s quite popular​ <a href="https://github.com/pocketbase/pocketbase/releases#:~:text=Releases%20%C2%B7%20pocketbase%2Fpocketbase%20,Issues%2018%20%C2%B7%20Pull" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">github.com</a>. However, it’s still in beta (version 0.x, not 1.0 yet) and the API is subject to change. It being SQLite means it’s good for moderate data sizes but not massive scale. Still, for quick prototyping or smaller apps, PocketBase is a <strong>rocket</strong> – extremely easy to deploy and no other dependencies. If you don’t need the full power of Postgres or the myriad features of Firebase, but want something simple you can host yourself, give PocketBase a look. (One caveat: no built-in cloud functions, you’d have to run custom code separately. It’s more comparable to the core of Appwrite without the functions, or a very slimmed down Supabase.)</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Nhost</strong> – Nhost is another open source Firebase alternative that takes a different approach: it’s built on <strong>Hasura (GraphQL)</strong> and Postgres. Essentially, Nhost gives you a ready-to-go GraphQL backend with auth and storage. If you prefer GraphQL over REST, this could be attractive. It’s like Supabase (since both are Postgres-based) but instead of REST + SQL, you get GraphQL APIs for everything. Nhost also has a cloud hosting and a CLI for local dev. It’s a bit more niche but has an active community. You might choose Nhost if you love GraphQL or if your frontend is already GraphQL-based and you want to skip writing a GraphQL server. Under the hood, it uses Hasura for the GraphQL engine and GoTrue for auth (same as Supabase’s auth)​. <a href="https://github.com/nhost/nhost#:~:text=Nhost%20is%20an%20open%20source,the%20following%20things%20in%20mind" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">github.com</a> <a href="https://github.com/nhost/nhost#:~:text=,Nhost%20CLI%20for%20local%20development" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">github.com</a>. So technologically, it’s like a close cousin to Supabase but with GraphQL. It has fewer stars (~8k) and is newer, but worth a mention for GraphQL fans.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Back4App</strong> – Back4App is basically a managed service around the open-source <strong>Parse Server</strong>. Parse (the platform Facebook open-sourced years ago) was one of the original BaaS solutions. Back4App took that and offers it as a service with additional tools. Parse uses a NoSQL JSON database (MongoDB typically) and has a GraphQL and REST API, plus cloud functions (in JS) and push notifications, etc. It’s quite feature-rich and proven (a lot of apps used Parse back in the day). Back4App also provides a web hosting integration (so you can host your front-end on their platform, similar to Firebase Hosting). If you liked Parse’s model or have legacy apps from Parse, Back4App is a good choice. However, the developer experience can feel a bit dated compared to newer players. Still, it’s stable and <strong>scales well</strong> (Back4App’s infrastructure can handle large apps). One thing it has is a web-based data browser and even a GUI for cloud code. If you’re coming from a Parse background, this could be comfortable. Otherwise, unless you specifically need something Parse offers, most new projects lean towards the other options mentioned.</li><li class="mb-1"><strong>Others</strong>: There are even more (e.g., <strong>AWS Amplify</strong> – Amazon’s answer to Firebase, combining AWS services with a dev-friendly interface; <strong>MongoDB Realm</strong> – now part of MongoDB Atlas, similar idea with MongoDB under the hood; and smaller ones like <strong>Xano</strong> (no-code backend) or <strong>Supabase’s ecosystem competitors</strong> like PlanetScale + Auth0 combos, etc.). Each has its pros/cons but covering them all would turn this post into a novel (well, it’s already quite long, haha).</li><li class="mb-1">The key is: the BaaS space is hot, and you have options. We focused on Firebase, Supabase, Appwrite as they are among the most popular and cover a nice spectrum (proprietary vs open, SQL vs NoSQL, self-host vs fully managed). PocketBase, Nhost, Back4App each slightly differ in target audience and technology:</li><li class="mb-1">PocketBase for lightweight self-contained needs,</li><li class="mb-1">Nhost for GraphQL aficionados,</li></ul><div></div><div>Back4App for Parse lovers or those who want a mBaaS with integrated web hosting.</div><div>You can explore these if the “big three” don’t float your boat. But if you asked me in 2025 which names I hear most in dev circles for quick backends, it’s still Firebase and Supabase at the top, with Appwrite and PocketBase rising quickly as well.</div><div></div><div><strong>Wrap Up:</strong> At the end of the day, whether you choose Firebase, Supabase, Appwrite, or another BaaS, you’re taking a load off your shoulders by not building everything from scratch. Each has its learning curve, but it’s <em>way</em> less effort than rolling your own authentication system or deploying your own database servers. As an Angular developer (or any frontend dev), using these services can feel like having a superpower – you can stand up a full-stack app mostly on your own.</div><div>My advice is to consider the factors we discussed: data model (SQL vs NoSQL), scalability, openness, community, and your personal comfort. Maybe even try a small proof-of-concept with each before committing. They all have free tiers, so spin up a Firebase project, a Supabase project, and an Appwrite instance, and play around. See which clicks with you.</div><div>And remember, <strong>no choice is permanent</strong>. If requirements change drastically, you can migrate later (it’s work, but not impossible). The important thing for an MVP or small project is to get something out there – these platforms let you do that fast.</div><div>Hopefully this overview gave you a clearer picture (and maybe a chuckle or two 😜). Now go forth and build some cool stuff! And whichever BaaS you pick, may your app never hit an unhandled exception in production. 🚀</div><div>Happy coding, <strong>ziomeczki</strong> (that’s “homies” in Polish – a little nod to my buddies who inspired this comparison). Until next time, keep it simple and ship it. Cheers! 🎉</div>', false, '2025-03-28T19:42:33+00:00', 'https://aqdbdmepncxxuanlymwr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/covers/ChatGPT%20Image%2011%20sie%202026,%2020_53_29.png', '[]'::jsonb),
  ('fa2c00ed-7cb6-409c-8e37-3b4c0236b8c1', 'a3b3cf93-6956-41c7-bec0-13f95dd42f26', 'Consistency is the key - super efficient blog app Part 2', 'In Chapter 2, we refine the project, fix issues, and add new features. We updated Angular to 18.0.6, resolving issues with withEventReplay(). The Admin role is now properly initialized using APP_INITIALIZER.  We restructured PostCardComponent into a fully reusable UI component with named slots. A new Role Directive allows flexible user permissions beyond just Admin.  For SSG, we implemented auto-generated routes using Firebase functions, though Angular 18.1 will soon handle this natively. We also introduced a GDPR cookie consent popup, storing settings in LocalStorage for now.  This chapter was a mix of problem-solving, learning, and fun. Stay tuned for Part 3—more optimizations ahead! 🚀', '<div><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">pre: to be sure if we are on same page, here is a branch for Part 1: </span><u style="color: rgb(17, 85, 204); background-color: transparent;"><a href="https://github.com/Lukecsharpwalker/angularBlogPublic/tree/Part--1" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Github repository to branch for Part--1</a></u></div><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Changes not included in part1:</span></div><ol><li><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Images for posts from assets, it’s temporary,</span></li><li><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Fixed styles, added styles for Quill, there is a collision with Tailwind,</span></li><li><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Change height of p-editor to 700px,</span></li><li><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Folder structure changes.</span></li></ol><h1 class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">CHAPTER 2 </span></h1><h2 class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">ASSUMPTIONS</span></h2><div class="ql-align-center"></div><div><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">I’ve created a project on GitHub, here is the roadmap for part 2. As you can see, we have some tasks in this ‘sprint’:</span></div><div class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;"><img src="https://lh7-us.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXdZISiTi8FtqPvv-eYLG_SJ4L8a71V5aLC1O-6Dzn9yCXYgJjkggCZfWcdRA9O3KAwazEHfSWBbDbSIjv4FXk2nYk0VdW0EdEWizqXYgobNlojR4wJxwhmvKQxrkWgcv0YsB-tW617EGJXLqMZYM3zuasz4?key=OvAySdI6JkikXf7BwYIHUQ" height="791" width="602"></span></div><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">Well done Microsoft, you really rock in UI and web development xD. Maybe it’s my fault I use heading as description. You guys forcing users to stick to good practises 😂</span></div><div></div><h2 class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">withEventReplay()</span></h2><div><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">In Part 1, we tried to use </span><strong style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;">withEventReplay()</strong><span style="color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent;"> and it caused an issue: </span></div><pre data-language="plaintext"><code class="plaintext">main.ts:5 ERROR TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading ''_ejsa'').
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">We will update the Angular version, we are on 18.0.2 and fresh is 18.0.6, so let’s update.&nbsp;</span></div><pre data-language="plaintext"><code class="plaintext">ng update @angular/cli @angular/core
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Now we can simply add </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">withEventReplay()</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> in </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">app.config.ts</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">provideClientHydration</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">withEventReplay</span>()).
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Time for a little test, I switched the network to 3G in Chrome Devtools. Spammed login button, woah! I found a bug, login opened a lot of times, that means:&nbsp;</span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">withEventReplay()</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> works!&nbsp;</span></div><div></div><div></div><h2 class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">ADMIN ROLE AND USSER AT APP INIT</span></h2><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">In the first part, there was an issue with the Admin role and Admin guard. I was trying hard for about 1h to move logic to </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">APP_INITIALIZER</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">. Without success, here I would like to thank </span><u style="color: rgb(17, 85, 204); background-color: transparent;"><a href="https://x.com/m_stefanczyk" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Mateusz Stefańczyk</a></u><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> for helping me with that. </span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">First of all, we will create inside </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">AuthService</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">, method: </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">&nbsp;</strong></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">getAdminStatusAndUser</span>(): <span class="hljs-title class_">Observable</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">boolean</span>&gt; {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Observable</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">observer</span>) =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title function_">onAuthStateChanged</span>(<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">auth</span>, <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">user</span>) =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (user) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">user$</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">set</span>(user);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;user.<span class="hljs-title function_">getIdTokenResult</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-attr">idTokenResult</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">IdTokenResult</span></span>) =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">isAdmin$</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">set</span>(idTokenResult.<span class="hljs-property">claims</span>[<span class="hljs-string">''admin''</span>] <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">boolean</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;observer.<span class="hljs-title function_">complete</span>();
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}).<span class="hljs-title function_">catch</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">error</span>) =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;observer.<span class="hljs-title function_">error</span>(error);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;});
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;} <span class="hljs-keyword">else</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">isAdmin$</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">set</span>(<span class="hljs-literal">false</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">user$</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">set</span>(<span class="hljs-literal">null</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;observer.<span class="hljs-title function_">complete</span>();
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;});
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;});
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">In an app folder, create new folder </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">utils, </strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">inside it </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">initialize-auth.ts</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">authInnitializer</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-attr">authService</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">AuthService</span></span>): <span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Observable</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">boolean</span>&gt; {
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> authService.<span class="hljs-title function_">getAdminStatusAndUser</span>();
}
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Finally in </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">providers </strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">array in </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">ApplicationConfig:</strong></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript">{
  <span class="hljs-attr">provide</span>: <span class="hljs-variable constant_">APP_INITIALIZER</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">useFactory</span>: authInnitializer,
  <span class="hljs-attr">deps</span>: [<span class="hljs-title class_">AuthService</span>],
  <span class="hljs-attr">multi</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>,
},
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Basically it’s it! Nice! Method name could be better, but let this name to grow in our mind.</span></div><div></div><h2 class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">PRESENTATIONAL CARD</span></h2><div class="ql-align-center"></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">We created a </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">BlogPostCard</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">, but it takes data as input. Why it’s not a great idea you will find out here </span><u style="color: rgb(17, 85, 204); background-color: transparent;"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YprehHgJRYA" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Mateusz Łędzewicz - Long road to reusable design system in Angular</a></u><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">.</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Maybe it will be a little bit of over engineering, but we are here to have fun, to test new solutions, to grow.&nbsp;</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Let’s rename </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">PostCardComponent </strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">to </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">CardComponent</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">. Get rid of:&nbsp;</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-meta">@Input</span>() post!: <span class="hljs-title class_">Post</span>;
<span class="hljs-meta">@Input</span>() first : <span class="hljs-built_in">boolean</span> = <span class="hljs-literal">false</span>;
</code></pre><div>The new <strong>card.component.html:</strong></div><pre data-language="xml"><code class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> [<span class="hljs-attr">ngClass</span>]=<span class="hljs-string">"first ? ''card-main'' : ''card-default''"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"post-card"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> [<span class="hljs-attr">ngClass</span>]=<span class="hljs-string">"first ? ''flex flex-col lg:flex-row'' : ''''"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">ng-content</span> <span class="hljs-attr">select</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"card-image"</span>&gt;</span><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">ng-content</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> [<span class="hljs-attr">ngClass</span>]=<span class="hljs-string">"first ? ''w-full lg:w-1/3 p-4'' : ''post-content''"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">ng-content</span> <span class="hljs-attr">select</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"card-header"</span>&gt;</span><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">ng-content</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">ng-content</span> <span class="hljs-attr">select</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"card-description"</span>&gt;</span><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">ng-content</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">It will contain 3 slots. For </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">image, hader and description.</strong></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">For use more than one slot, we need to enable:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-attr">schemas</span>: [<span class="hljs-variable constant_">CUSTOM_ELEMENTS_SCHEMA</span>]
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">In </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">@Component</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> configuration.</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">The </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">post-list.component.html</strong></div><pre data-language="xml"><code class="xml">@for (post of posts$ | async; track post.id; let f = $first; let i = $index) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">blog-post-card</span> [<span class="hljs-attr">first</span>]=<span class="hljs-string">"f"</span> [<span class="hljs-attr">ngClass</span>]=<span class="hljs-string">"f ? ''col-span-1 md:col-span-2 lg:col-span-3'' : ''''"</span> &gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">card-image</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">img</span> <span class="hljs-attr">src</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"{{i + 1}}.png"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">alt</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"Post Image"</span> [<span class="hljs-attr">ngClass</span>]=<span class="hljs-string">"f ? ''w-full lg:w-2/3'' : ''post-image''"</span> /&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">card-image</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">card-header</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">h2</span> [<span class="hljs-attr">routerLink</span>]=<span class="hljs-string">"[''details'', post.id, i]"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"post-title underline cursor-pointer"</span>&gt;</span>{{ post.title }}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">h2</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">card-header</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">card-description</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">p</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"post-summary"</span>&gt;</span>{{ post.description }}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">p</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">card-description</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">blog-post-card</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">And that’s it, now we have a pure UI component. Can be reused whenever we want and still we don’t need to put a lot of effort into displaying data.&nbsp;</span></div><div></div><div></div><h2 class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">ROLE DIRECTIVE</span></h2><div class="ql-align-center"></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Now we are only checking if the user is </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Admin</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">, but what if we want to have more writers, maybe some </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Moderators</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">.&nbsp;</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">In </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">/src/app/shared/_enums</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">, create </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Roles</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> enum.</span></div><pre data-language="plain"><code class="plain">ng g enum Roles
</code></pre><div></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">enum</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span> {
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable constant_">READER</span> = <span class="hljs-string">''Reader''</span>,
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable constant_">MODERATOR</span> = <span class="hljs-string">''Moderator''</span>,
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable constant_">WRITER</span> = <span class="hljs-string">''Writer''</span>,
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable constant_">ADMIN</span> = <span class="hljs-string">''Admin''</span>
}
</code></pre><div></div><div><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">User</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> interface form Firebase does not include properties like </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Role </strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">and </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Permission</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">. We will create a new interface, based on Firebase </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">User</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">. In </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">app/shared/_models</strong></div><pre data-language="plain"><code class="plain">ng g interface blog-user
</code></pre><div></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">interface</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">BlogUser</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">extends</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">User</span> {
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-attr">roles</span>: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>[];
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-attr">permissions</span>: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>[];
}
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">In </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">AuthService</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> add three methods:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">hasRole</span>(<span class="hljs-attr">role</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span>): <span class="hljs-built_in">boolean</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> !!<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">user$</span>()?.<span class="hljs-property">roles</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">includes</span>(role);
&nbsp;}

&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title function_">hasPermission</span>(<span class="hljs-attr">permission</span>: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>): <span class="hljs-built_in">boolean</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> !!<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">user$</span>()?.<span class="hljs-property">permissions</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">includes</span>(permission);
&nbsp;}
<span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">getRoles</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-attr">idTokenResult</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">IdTokenResult</span></span>) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (idTokenResult.<span class="hljs-property">claims</span>[<span class="hljs-string">''admin''</span>]) <span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">user$</span>()!.<span class="hljs-property">roles</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">push</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span>.<span class="hljs-property">ADMIN</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (idTokenResult.<span class="hljs-property">claims</span>[<span class="hljs-string">''moderator''</span>]) <span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">user$</span>()!.<span class="hljs-property">roles</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">push</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span>.<span class="hljs-property">MODERATOR</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (idTokenResult.<span class="hljs-property">claims</span>[<span class="hljs-string">''writer''</span>]) <span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">user$</span>()!.<span class="hljs-property">roles</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">push</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span>.<span class="hljs-property">WRITER</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">user$</span>()!.<span class="hljs-property">roles</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">push</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span>.<span class="hljs-property">READER</span>);
&nbsp;}
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">And update this one:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">getAdminStatusAndUser</span>(): <span class="hljs-title class_">Observable</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">boolean</span>&gt; {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Observable</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">observer</span>) =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title function_">onAuthStateChanged</span>(<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">auth</span>, <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">user</span>) =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (user) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">user$</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">set</span>(user <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">BlogUser</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">user$</span>()!.<span class="hljs-property">roles</span> = [];
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;user.<span class="hljs-title function_">getIdTokenResult</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-attr">idTokenResult</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">IdTokenResult</span></span>) =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">getRoles</span>(idTokenResult);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;observer.<span class="hljs-title function_">complete</span>();
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}).<span class="hljs-title function_">catch</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">error</span>) =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;observer.<span class="hljs-title function_">error</span>(error);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;});
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;} <span class="hljs-keyword">else</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">isAdmin$</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">set</span>(<span class="hljs-literal">false</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">user$</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">set</span>(<span class="hljs-literal">null</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;observer.<span class="hljs-title function_">complete</span>();
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;});
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;});
&nbsp;}
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">I don’t like this ifs. We set custom claims like this</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">setCustomUserClaims</span>(res.<span class="hljs-property">uid</span>, { <span class="hljs-attr">admin</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">true</span> })
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">but I think we could do it like this&nbsp;</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">setCustomUserClaims</span>(res.<span class="hljs-property">uid</span>, { roles : {<span class="hljs-attr">admin</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>} })
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">And we''ll be able to just map </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">roles</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> -&gt; </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">roles</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">. Added it to the backlog. I think I should write the whole solution and then just rewrite it using solutions that come to my mind during development, ehh I need to carry one with this shame.&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></div><div></div><div class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"><img src="https://lh7-us.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXdK81lHbyYV7Yfnsz4ovnqJasE0eBR5DY6_Lmbup78_2YWID1PSycDDIXvCMRjtgJ5luMGMIFFVreN8yykOipoIANVW9SC4xh4JFJyxaatI4jNGwc9QK7gaG9WuOpqHwceKQ6Xun7o5EanQWse-Fs8NYU0o?key=OvAySdI6JkikXf7BwYIHUQ" height="601" width="602"></span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Hey young Jim Carrey! That''s a really nice curved screen!&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></div><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">We can jump to the app</span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> _shared</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> folder. And create the </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">_directive </strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">folder, and inside it.</span></div><pre data-language="plain"><code class="plain">ng g d has-role
</code></pre><div></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-meta">@Directive</span>({
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-attr">selector</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''[hasRole]''</span>,
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-attr">standalone</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>
})
<span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">HasRoleDirective</span> {
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-meta">@Input</span>() <span class="hljs-keyword">set</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">hasRole</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-attr">roles</span>: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>[]</span>) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">updateView</span>(roles);
&nbsp;}

&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> authService = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">AuthService</span>);
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> templateRef = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">TemplateRef</span>);
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> viewContainer = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">ViewContainerRef</span>);

&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">updateView</span>(<span class="hljs-attr">roles</span>: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>[]): <span class="hljs-built_in">void</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> user = <span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">authService</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">user$</span>();
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (user?.<span class="hljs-property">roles</span>) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> hasRole = user.<span class="hljs-property">roles</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">some</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">role</span> =&gt;</span> roles.<span class="hljs-title function_">includes</span>(role));
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (hasRole) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">viewContainer</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">createEmbeddedView</span>(<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">templateRef</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;} <span class="hljs-keyword">else</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">viewContainer</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">clear</span>();
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;} <span class="hljs-keyword">else</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">viewContainer</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">clear</span>();
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}
&nbsp;}
}
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Great! We can add </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">delete</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> button in </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">comments</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> HTML:</span></div><pre data-language="xml"><code class="xml">&nbsp;@for (comment of comments$(); track comment.id) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"comment mb-4 p-4 bg-tertiary bg-opacity-20 rounded-lg shadow-sm flex justify-between items-center"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">p</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"text-gray-800 mb-2"</span>&gt;</span>{{ comment.content }}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">p</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">p</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"text-sm text-gray-600"</span>&gt;</span>- {{ comment.author }}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">p</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">button</span> (<span class="hljs-attr">click</span>)=<span class="hljs-string">"deleteComment(comment.id!)"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"text-red-500 hover:text-red-700"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">svg</span> <span class="hljs-attr">xmlns</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"h-6 w-6"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">fill</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"none"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">viewBox</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"0 0 24 24"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">stroke</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"currentColor"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">path</span> <span class="hljs-attr">stroke-linecap</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"round"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">stroke-linejoin</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"round"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">stroke-width</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"2"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">d</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12"</span> /&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">svg</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">button</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;}
&nbsp;@empty {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">p</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"text-gray-800"</span>&gt;</span>No comments yet.<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">p</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;}
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Also, I removed </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">@if</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> and replaced </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">@else</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> with </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">@empty</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">.</span></div><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Little bit changes in TS file:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">CommentsComponent</span>&nbsp;{
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-meta">@Input</span>() postId!: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>;
&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span> = <span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span>;
&nbsp;comments$ = input&lt;<span class="hljs-title class_">Comment</span>[] | <span class="hljs-literal">undefined</span>&gt;();

&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> apiService = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">ReaderApiService</span>);

&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title function_">deleteComment</span>(<span class="hljs-attr">commentId</span>: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>): <span class="hljs-built_in">void</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">apiService</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">deleteComment</span>(commentId, <span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">postId</span>)
&nbsp;}
}
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">And in the </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">ReaderApiService</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">deleteComment</span>(<span class="hljs-attr">commentId</span>: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">postId</span>: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>): <span class="hljs-built_in">void</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> commentRef = <span class="hljs-title function_">doc</span>(<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">firestore</span>, <span class="hljs-string">`<span class="hljs-subst">${Collections.POST}</span>/<span class="hljs-subst">${postId}</span>/<span class="hljs-subst">${Collections.COMMENT}</span>/<span class="hljs-subst">${commentId}</span>`</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title function_">deleteDoc</span>(commentRef);
&nbsp;}
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">And least, we need to add rule on </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">DB</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript">allow <span class="hljs-attr">delete</span>: <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> request.<span class="hljs-property">auth</span>.<span class="hljs-property">token</span>.<span class="hljs-property">admin</span> == <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>;
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Edit: I forgot to update guards and logins XD.&nbsp;</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">In </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">AuthService</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">, every cast admin role to boolean, replace with:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">getRoles</span>(idTokenResult);
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">get rid of:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-attr">isAdmin$</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">WritableSignal</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">boolean</span>&gt; = <span class="hljs-title function_">signal</span>(<span class="hljs-literal">false</span>);
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Fixed </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">authGuard:</strong></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-attr">authGuard</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">CanMatchFn</span> = (): <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">boolean</span> =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> authService = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">AuthService</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> router = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">Router</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (authService.<span class="hljs-title function_">user$</span>()?.<span class="hljs-property">roles</span>?.<span class="hljs-title function_">includes</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span>.<span class="hljs-property">ADMIN</span>)) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>;
&nbsp;&nbsp;} <span class="hljs-keyword">else</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;router.<span class="hljs-title function_">navigate</span>([<span class="hljs-string">''/posts''</span>]);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-literal">false</span>;
&nbsp;&nbsp;}
};
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Yeah, working as a charm!</span></div><div></div><h2 class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">AUTO GENERATED ROUTES FOR SSG</span></h2><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Maybe you remember this file: </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">routes.txt</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> - it contains all our routes that should be pre rendered - great feature, but I wasn’t sure - how to auto generate it, in Angular 18.1 this issue will be covered. We want to have fun, so let’s do it another way now.</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Another big thanks for </span><u style="color: rgb(17, 85, 204); background-color: transparent;"><a href="https://x.com/m_stefanczyk" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Mateusz Stefańczyk</a></u><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> who helps me a lot with this task. He confirmed my belief that it should be done in </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">server.ts</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> and showed me how to achieve this!</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">We need all </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">posts ids</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">. But I think it’s an overkill to download all of the posts to get only ids. So let’s create new </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">document</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> which will only contains posts Ids:</span></div><div class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"><img src="https://lh7-us.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXclkTUJ5q7O0lHUBA_-kIWvk7uwqHJ0M5K7G5-_kxwuR1coOydKGEtXcu1q3CDTnwFMGk2O0Rj7CozO9RILcCr5AYGWgvn8Lqf8ggpNzh4Tx4b-sHbrh4H-m6Yrc8NkjNuiDVMVC3C_ilIjbOamPr0R_RSr?key=OvAySdI6JkikXf7BwYIHUQ" height="455" width="602"></span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Save</span></div><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Update </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">AdminApiService</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-meta">@Injectable</span>()
<span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">AdminApiService</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> firestore = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">Firestore</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title function_">addPost</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-attr">post</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">Post</span></span>) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> newTaskRef = <span class="hljs-title function_">doc</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">collection</span>(<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">firestore</span>, <span class="hljs-title class_">Collections</span>.<span class="hljs-property">POST</span>));
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title function_">setDoc</span>(newTaskRef, post).<span class="hljs-title function_">finally</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''Document written with ID: ''</span>, newTaskRef.<span class="hljs-property">id</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> newTaskRefIds = <span class="hljs-title function_">doc</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">collection</span>(<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">firestore</span>, <span class="hljs-title class_">Collections</span>.<span class="hljs-property">POSTSIDS</span>));
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title function_">setDoc</span>(newTaskRefIds, {<span class="hljs-attr">id</span>: post.<span class="hljs-property">id</span>}).<span class="hljs-title function_">finally</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''id added''</span>, newTaskRefIds.<span class="hljs-property">id</span>)
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;});
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;});
&nbsp;&nbsp;}
}
</code></pre><div></div><pre data-language="plain"><code class="plain">core.mjs:6677 ERROR FirebaseError: Function setDoc() called with invalid data. Unsupported field value: undefined (found in field id in document postsIds/Zvq9tK0m5BB9KP3i4lw0)
</code></pre><div><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">GREAT</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> 👍</span></div><div></div><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Id should come from </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">{id: newTaskRef.id} </strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">&nbsp;not </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">{id: post.id}</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">.&nbsp;</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Let’s jump into our </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">server.ts</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> created with the Angular app. BTW Angular CLI also take care about scripts and out of a box we have this script:</span></div><pre data-language="plaintext"><code class="plaintext">"serve:ssr:AngularBlogApp": "node dist/angular-blog-app/server/server.mjs"
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">So we can just run</span></div><pre data-language="plaintext"><code class="plaintext">ng build
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Then </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">ng</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">serve</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">, and we can test our server.</span></div><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">We will need an Admin</span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> SDK</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> to perform this action. We can find it in </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Project settings -&gt; Service accounts</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">.&nbsp;</span></div><div class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"><img src="https://lh7-us.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXci_rPmLbLTlYBfWgHrdB73FjoU8Fi8ZvU3iZ00PYgO6vjK8JesIdWtegBZJIyzpI4pvnYqnMvxsJ5gg-vx1FegqJ8OGG3tcD543lwT6ZsvFZxkiJG0HLtXgLkDW2T3yGwbFAl5cUwq1-W2pFL0r_NaZOrT?key=OvAySdI6JkikXf7BwYIHUQ" height="467" width="602"></span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Generate new private key.</span></div><div></div><div></div><div></div><div></div><div class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"><img src="https://lh7-us.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXcB7sMsbYBLXaHJsWxM7SPYqyzeE-3tHnlcezM9MssrEFq5OKka6UvbVajk2wkhofXvVOUf7wpsorEDAEuMIeKBiwU9n3TAMKqwRXrwvwVIp_r4O46lCV8YjYAbn_Ghm9Wyfy1nn5AJTYvVJvjfaCialMfd?key=OvAySdI6JkikXf7BwYIHUQ" height="241" width="578"></span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Sorry guys! I need some privacy right now!</span></div><div class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"><img src="https://lh7-us.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXd_j0A-wPwMhL0RMJokCco6s5Ahmd1ehRw-BPOk4j4juQEh-6k1s5eyQkIFU6_DOA6BcPNAtT9vVJP4Ek2WPdCt7nxPtjosnDBYzlFBXwu8lOTJErNu4UuktAUeXVXDpK48yjE-r2MOCeU61QSpUotWOsxR?key=OvAySdI6JkikXf7BwYIHUQ" height="601" width="602"></span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Some strange ice bucket challenge.</span></div><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Nice, firebase gave us a nice brick of code. Let''s add it to our </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">project</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> and to the </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">.gitignore</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> file.</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">OK! I tried to use </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Firebase</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> admin. Really tried. It took me the whole day. Without any success. Still getting this error:</span></div><pre data-language="plaintext"><code class="plaintext">this._gaxModule.GrpcClient is not a constructor
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">I tried to make a new </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">node.js</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> project, everything working as expected.&nbsp;Added config from angular - still working.</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">I tried to make a new Angular project, was’t working.</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">I replaced the Angular server with a working </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">node.js </strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">server - not working. WTF?! I think something is buggy, I can''t access </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">firebase admin</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> through admin </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">server.ts</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">. FUUUU. I’m passing.&nbsp;</span></div><div class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"><img src="https://lh7-us.googleusercontent.com/docsz/AD_4nXe2wUMYa3bcWLFHUI7iKs7RmWipaa4SMj2ZKO4m8J9UFs0cifpkyWm2E1MKfTfj4qnqFp2OhwrvW9VfGC6V0ukw4bOkFGYivh3DQdAMHcqm4RaxjiCDch8oPIbOm6YXhM5MdpGpn7nwHFvpxnCbWS-xG9E2?key=OvAySdI6JkikXf7BwYIHUQ" height="344" width="602"></span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">But something there is blinking, at the end of a tunnel. It’s that hope? Yeah it is! I just found a solution - why not to use functions? Let’s try, hopefully my windows aren''t high but I’m, a little bit.&nbsp;</span></div><div></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> * <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> functions <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">''firebase-functions''</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> * <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> admin <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">''firebase-admin''</span>;
admin.<span class="hljs-title function_">initializeApp</span>();
<span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> fetchPostsFromFirestore = functions.<span class="hljs-property">https</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">onRequest</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">async</span> (request, response) =&gt; {
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">try</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> db = admin.<span class="hljs-title function_">firestore</span>();
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> snapshot = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> db.<span class="hljs-title function_">collection</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''postsIds''</span>).<span class="hljs-title function_">get</span>();
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> posts = snapshot.<span class="hljs-property">docs</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">map</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">doc</span> =&gt;</span> doc.<span class="hljs-title function_">data</span>());
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;response.<span class="hljs-title function_">status</span>(<span class="hljs-number">200</span>).<span class="hljs-title function_">json</span>(posts);
&nbsp;&nbsp;} <span class="hljs-keyword">catch</span> (error) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">error</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''Error fetching posts from Firestore:''</span>, error);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;response.<span class="hljs-title function_">status</span>(<span class="hljs-number">500</span>).<span class="hljs-title function_">json</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">status</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''error''</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">message</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''Failed to fetch posts from Firestore.''</span> });
&nbsp;&nbsp;}
});
<span class="hljs-comment">// Firebase function URL</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> firebaseFunctionUrl = <span class="hljs-string">''rerekumkum''</span>;
<span class="hljs-comment">// Function to fetch data from Firebase function</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">fetchPosts</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">try</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> response = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">fetch</span>(firebaseFunctionUrl);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (!response.<span class="hljs-property">ok</span>) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">throw</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Error</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''Network response was not ok ''</span> + response.<span class="hljs-property">statusText</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-attr">data</span>: [{<span class="hljs-attr">id</span>:<span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>}] = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> response.<span class="hljs-title function_">json</span>();
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> routes = data.<span class="hljs-title function_">map</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">post</span>) =&gt;</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> (<span class="hljs-string">`/posts/<span class="hljs-subst">${post.id}</span>`</span>)
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}).<span class="hljs-title function_">join</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''\n''</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;fs.<span class="hljs-title function_">writeFileSync</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''routes.txt''</span>, routes, <span class="hljs-string">''utf8''</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;} <span class="hljs-keyword">catch</span> (error) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">error</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''Error fetching posts:''</span>, error);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">throw</span> error;
&nbsp;&nbsp;}
}
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">TADAH! </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">routes.txt</strong></div><pre data-language="plain"><code class="plain">/posts/gGxlt9fWhDngExOWpInc
/posts/68PPeQns9OMQHJB7p5aW
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">We need a little bit different url and url for main page</span></div><pre data-language="plain"><code class="plain">/posts
/posts/
posts/details/2lkZK8w4QWvjEIsduqrh
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">I hope you can fix it yourself ;)</span></div><h2 class="ql-align-center"></h2><h2 class="ql-align-center"><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">GDRP COOKIES POPUP</span></h2><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">I hope it’s a good name. To be honest I never think about how it works. I bet it’s saving settings in cookies, if you store cookie acceptance in cookies you also need permission to store acceptance? Cookcepction.&nbsp;</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">So for now we don’t carry any cookies, just create a template and store settings in </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">LocalStorage</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">. We’ll use our </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Dynamic Dialog</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> and maybe </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Card</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">. Let''s move the Card to the shared folder.&nbsp;</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">We can start with re-arrange the modal component. For now we have - </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Login </strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">header as just HTML value and only one button, cancel button.</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Config interface will be needed:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">interface</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">ModalConfig</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;title?: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>;
&nbsp;&nbsp;primaryButton?: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>;
&nbsp;&nbsp;secondaryButton?: <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>;
}
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">Also, status for reference that will be caught.&nbsp;So we will know if the user clicked some kind of </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">accept</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> button or </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">cancel</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;"> button.&nbsp;</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">export interface ModalStatus {</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">&nbsp;&nbsp;status: ModalStatusEnum</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">}</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">enum</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">ModalStatusEnum</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable constant_">ACCEPTED</span> = <span class="hljs-string">''accepted''</span>,
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable constant_">REJECTED</span> = <span class="hljs-string">''rejected''</span>,
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable constant_">CLOSED</span> = <span class="hljs-string">''closed''</span>
}
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">A little bit change need to be done in </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">DynamicDialogService</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">DynamicDialogService</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> envInjector = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">EnvironmentInjector</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> componentRef!: <span class="hljs-title class_">ComponentRef</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title class_">DynamicDialogComponent</span>&gt;;
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> closeRef$ = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Subject</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title class_">ModalStatusEnum</span>&gt;();

&nbsp;&nbsp;openDialog&lt;C <span class="hljs-keyword">extends</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Type</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">unknown</span>&gt;&gt;(<span class="hljs-attr">component</span>: C, <span class="hljs-attr">viewContainerRef</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">ViewContainerRef</span>, modalConfig?: <span class="hljs-title class_">ModalConfig</span>): <span class="hljs-title class_">Subject</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title class_">ModalStatusEnum</span>&gt; {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">componentRef</span> =
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;viewContainerRef.<span class="hljs-title function_">createComponent</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">DynamicDialogComponent</span>, { <span class="hljs-attr">environmentInjector</span>: <span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">envInjector</span> });
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">componentRef</span>.<span class="hljs-property">instance</span>.<span class="hljs-property">component</span> = component;
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">componentRef</span>.<span class="hljs-property">instance</span>.<span class="hljs-property">modalConfig</span> = modalConfig;
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">closeRef$</span>;
&nbsp;&nbsp;};
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title function_">closeDialog</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-attr">status</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">ModalStatusEnum</span></span>) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">componentRef</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">destroy</span>();
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">closeRef$</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">next</span>(status);
&nbsp;&nbsp;};
</code></pre><div></div><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">And </span><strong style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">DynamicDialogComponent</strong><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">:</span></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">DynamicDialogComponent</span>&lt;C = <span class="hljs-built_in">unknown</span>&gt; <span class="hljs-keyword">implements</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">OnInit</span>{
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-meta">@Input</span>() component!: <span class="hljs-title class_">Type</span>&lt;C&gt;;
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-meta">@Input</span>() modalConfig?: <span class="hljs-title class_">ModalConfig</span>;
&nbsp;&nbsp;divEl = viewChild.<span class="hljs-title function_">required</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''dynamicComponentContainer''</span>, {<span class="hljs-attr">read</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">ViewContainerRef</span>});
&nbsp;&nbsp;dynamicDialogService = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">DynamicDialogService</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;viewContainerRef = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">ViewContainerRef</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title class_">ModalStatusEnum</span> = <span class="hljs-title class_">ModalStatusEnum</span>;
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title function_">ngOnInit</span>(): <span class="hljs-built_in">void</span> {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">divEl</span>()) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">divEl</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">createComponent</span>(<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">component</span>);
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}
&nbsp;&nbsp;}
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-title function_">closeDialog</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-attr">status</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">ModalStatusEnum</span></span>) {
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">dynamicDialogService</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">closeDialog</span>(status);
&nbsp;&nbsp;}
}
</code></pre><div><span style="color: rgb(14, 14, 14); background-color: transparent;">And UI:</span></div><pre data-language="xml"><code class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"relative z-10"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">aria-labelledby</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"modal-title"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">role</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"dialog"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">aria-modal</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"true"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"fixed inset-0 bg-gray-500 bg-opacity-75 transition-opacity"</span>&gt;</span><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"fixed inset-0 z-10 w-screen overflow-y-auto"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"flex min-h-full items-end justify-center p-4 text-center sm:items-center sm:p-0"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"relative transform overflow-hidden rounded-lg bg-white text-left shadow-xl transition-all sm:my-8 sm:w-full sm:max-w-lg"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"bg-white px-4 pb-4 pt-5 sm:p-6 sm:pb-4"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"mt-3 text-center sm:ml-4 sm:mt-0 sm:text-left"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">h3</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"text-base font-semibold leading-6 text-gray-900"</span> <span class="hljs-attr">id</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"modal-title"</span>&gt;</span>{{ modalConfig?.title || '''' }}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">h3</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">hr</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"h-px w-100 my-1 bg-gray-100 border-0 dark:bg-gray-700"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span> <span class="hljs-attr">class</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"mt-2"</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">ng-template</span> #<span class="hljs-attr">dynamicComponentContainer</span>&gt;</span><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">ng-template</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
