  ('dbfcd015-390f-4cd6-bb62-f0218354da3e', 'a3b3cf93-6956-41c7-bec0-13f95dd42f26', 'Super performant Blog App in Angular 19 with SSG SSR and Hydration Part 6: Playwright', 'This blog post walks through setting up a local Supabase instance with helpful new npm scripts—like schema:pull, db:seed, and start:local—and integrating Playwright via ng add playwright-ng-schematics to enable robust E2E testing with cookie consent handling. It further enhances test reliability by adding custom helpers and a comprehensive Playwright spec that verifies both UI elements and backend tag data, and shows how to conditionally trigger CI workflows when commit messages include “E2E.”', '<div>Hello, new scripts were added to the package.json</div><pre data-language="bash"><code class="javascript"><span class="hljs-string">"schema:pull"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"find supabase/migrations -name ''*_remote_schema.sql'' -delete &amp;&amp; supabase db pull --db-url $PG_EXPORT_URL"</span>,
<span class="hljs-string">"db:createSeed"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"scripts/create-seed.sh"</span>,
<span class="hljs-string">"db:seed"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"npx @snaplet/seed init"</span>,
</code></pre><div>and</div><pre data-language="bash"><code class="javascript"><span class="hljs-string">"start:local"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"ng serve --configuration=local"</span>,
</code></pre><div>So all of you now can run this app locally with a local Supabase instance.</div><div>The<strong> seed.sql </strong>file contains a seed with all tags; also, two users are included, one of whom has an admin role! I hope it''ll help with the E2E CI pipe.</div><h2 class="ql-align-center">Add Plawright to the project</h2><pre data-language="plain"><code class="javascript">ng add playwright-ng-schematics
</code></pre><div><em>What this does behind the scenes</em>:</div><ul><li>Installs @playwright/test and the browser binaries.</li><li>Creates playwright.config.ts.</li><li>Adds an e2e architect target in angular.json, so ng e2e becomes a wrapper around npx playwright test.</li><li>Generates a starter spec in e2e/example.spec.ts.</li></ul><div class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://aqdbdmepncxxuanlymwr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/part6pw//Xnapper-2025-06-10-17.04.40.jpg" alt="files created by PW init"></div><div>We need to extend configuration a little bit:</div><pre data-language="diff"><code class="javascript"><span class="hljs-string">"e2e"</span>: {
  <span class="hljs-string">"builder"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"playwright-ng-schematics:playwright"</span>,
  <span class="hljs-string">"options"</span>: {
    <span class="hljs-string">"devServerTarget"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"angularblogapp:serve"</span>
  },
  <span class="hljs-string">"configurations"</span>: {
    <span class="hljs-string">"production"</span>: {
      <span class="hljs-string">"devServerTarget"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"angularblogapp:serve:production"</span>
    },
    <span class="hljs-string">"local"</span>: {
      <span class="hljs-string">"devServerTarget"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"angularblogapp:serve:local"</span>
    }
  }
}
</code></pre><div>And the new NPM script:</div><pre data-language="plain"><code class="javascript"><span class="hljs-string">"e2e:local"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"ng e2e -c local"</span>
</code></pre><div>It looks like Playwright has started, with some issues, but it''s working.</div><div class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://aqdbdmepncxxuanlymwr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/part6pw//Xnapper-2025-06-10-18.12.17.jpg" alt="Playwright GUI"></div><div>As expected, the example test fails.</div><div>Let''s stop here for a while. I tried to run.</div><pre data-language="plain"><code class="javascript">npx playwright test --ui
</code></pre><div>But I haven''t seen any tests; I''ve tried a few things. After that, I restarted my computer, and that helped me. Later had the same issue, read that it could be caused by an internal terminal of IDE. For now it''s working.</div><pre data-language="javascript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> { expect, test } <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">''@playwright/test''</span>;

<span class="hljs-title function_">test</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''has title and handles cookie consent''</span>, <span class="hljs-title function_">async</span> ({ page }) =&gt; {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> page.<span class="hljs-title function_">goto</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''/''</span>);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">expect</span>(page).<span class="hljs-title function_">toHaveTitle</span>(<span class="hljs-regexp">/AngularBlogApp/</span>);

  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> cookieConsentDialog = page
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">getByLabel</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''Cookie Consent''</span>)
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">locator</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''div''</span>)
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">filter</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">hasText</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''Cookie Consent Consent''</span> })
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">nth</span>(<span class="hljs-number">1</span>);

  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">expect</span>(cookieConsentDialog).<span class="hljs-title function_">toBeVisible</span>();

  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> allowAllButton = page.<span class="hljs-title function_">getByRole</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''button''</span>, { <span class="hljs-attr">name</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''Allow All''</span> });
  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> allowAllButton.<span class="hljs-title function_">click</span>();

  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">expect</span>(cookieConsentDialog).<span class="hljs-property">not</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">toBeVisible</span>();
});
</code></pre><div>Let''s run it:</div><div class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://aqdbdmepncxxuanlymwr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/part6pw//Xnapper-2025-06-11-08.44.29.jpg" alt="playwright GUI with test list"></div><div>I have some issues with the <strong>Webkit</strong>, the issue comes from macOS, unfortunately.</div><pre data-language="plain"><code class="javascript"><span class="hljs-title class_">Library</span>/<span class="hljs-title class_">Caches</span>/ms-playwright/webkit-<span class="hljs-number">2182</span>/pw_run.<span class="hljs-property">sh</span>: line <span class="hljs-number">7</span>:  <span class="hljs-number">6097</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Bus</span> <span class="hljs-attr">error</span>: <span class="hljs-number">10</span>           
<span class="hljs-variable constant_">DYLD_FRAMEWORK_PATH</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"$DYLIB_PATH"</span> <span class="hljs-variable constant_">DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH</span>=<span class="hljs-string">"$DYLIB_PATH"</span> <span class="hljs-string">"$PLAYWRIGHT"</span> <span class="hljs-string">"$@"</span>
</code></pre><div>But PW UI working as expected:</div><div class="ql-align-center"><img src="https://aqdbdmepncxxuanlymwr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/part6pw//Xnapper-2025-06-11-08.57.34.jpg" alt="Playwright UI tests passed"></div><div>It looks like everything is quite fine!</div><div>At every test fire, Cookie Pop-Up will appear. Let''s add a helper function.</div><pre data-language="javascript"><code class="typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> { <span class="hljs-title class_">Page</span>, expect } <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">''@playwright/test''</span>;

<span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">acceptCookies</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-attr">page</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">Page</span></span>): <span class="hljs-title class_">Promise</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">void</span>&gt; {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> cookieConsentDialog = page
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">getByLabel</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''Cookie Consent''</span>)
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">locator</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''div''</span>)
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">filter</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">hasText</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''Cookie Consent Consent''</span> })
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">nth</span>(<span class="hljs-number">1</span>);

  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">expect</span>(cookieConsentDialog).<span class="hljs-title function_">toBeVisible</span>();

  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> allowAllButton = page.<span class="hljs-title function_">getByRole</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''button''</span>, { <span class="hljs-attr">name</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''Allow All''</span> });
  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> allowAllButton.<span class="hljs-title function_">click</span>();

  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">expect</span>(cookieConsentDialog).<span class="hljs-property">not</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">toBeVisible</span>();
}

</code></pre><div>We also need a test that will tell us the whole truth. If the Supabase API is working and returning data correctly, if migration is working, if seed is working. It''s the key to integration tests. We can check if tags are properly loaded from the DB.</div><pre data-language="javascript"><code class="yaml"><span class="hljs-string">import</span> { <span class="hljs-string">test</span>, <span class="hljs-string">expect</span> } <span class="hljs-string">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">''@playwright/test''</span><span class="hljs-string">;</span>
<span class="hljs-string">import</span> { <span class="hljs-string">acceptCookies</span> } <span class="hljs-string">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">''./helpers/cookie-consent.helper''</span><span class="hljs-string">;</span>
<span class="hljs-string">import</span> { <span class="hljs-string">waitForAngularToLoad</span>, <span class="hljs-string">waitForApiCall</span> } <span class="hljs-string">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">''./helpers/debug.helper''</span><span class="hljs-string">;</span>

<span class="hljs-string">test.describe(''Tags</span> <span class="hljs-string">Display</span> <span class="hljs-string">and</span> <span class="hljs-string">API'',</span> <span class="hljs-string">()</span> <span class="hljs-string">=&gt;</span> {
  <span class="hljs-string">test(''should</span> <span class="hljs-string">display</span> <span class="hljs-string">tags</span> <span class="hljs-string">and</span> <span class="hljs-string">verify</span> <span class="hljs-string">API</span> <span class="hljs-string">call''</span>, <span class="hljs-string">async</span> <span class="hljs-string">(</span>{ <span class="hljs-string">page</span> }<span class="hljs-string">)</span> <span class="hljs-string">=&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-attr">const apiRequests:</span> <span class="hljs-string">Array&lt;</span>{
      <span class="hljs-attr">url:</span> <span class="hljs-string">string;</span>
      <span class="hljs-attr">method:</span> <span class="hljs-string">string;</span>
      <span class="hljs-attr">headers:</span> <span class="hljs-string">Record&lt;string</span>, <span class="hljs-string">string&gt;;</span>
      <span class="hljs-string">status?:</span> <span class="hljs-string">number;</span>
    }<span class="hljs-string">&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-string">=</span> []<span class="hljs-string">;</span>

    <span class="hljs-string">page.on(''request''</span>, <span class="hljs-string">(request)</span> <span class="hljs-string">=&gt;</span> {
      <span class="hljs-string">const</span> <span class="hljs-string">url</span> <span class="hljs-string">=</span> <span class="hljs-string">request.url();</span>
      <span class="hljs-string">if</span> <span class="hljs-string">(</span>
        <span class="hljs-string">url.includes(''/rest/v1/tags'')</span> <span class="hljs-string">||</span>
        <span class="hljs-string">url.includes(''supabase'')</span> <span class="hljs-string">||</span>
        <span class="hljs-string">url.includes(''tag'')</span>
      <span class="hljs-string">)</span> {
        <span class="hljs-string">apiRequests.push(</span>{
          <span class="hljs-string">url</span>,
          <span class="hljs-attr">method:</span> <span class="hljs-string">request.method()</span>,
          <span class="hljs-attr">headers:</span> <span class="hljs-string">request.headers()</span>,
        }<span class="hljs-string">);</span>
      }
    }<span class="hljs-string">);</span>

    <span class="hljs-string">page.on(''response''</span>, <span class="hljs-string">(response)</span> <span class="hljs-string">=&gt;</span> {
      <span class="hljs-string">const</span> <span class="hljs-string">url</span> <span class="hljs-string">=</span> <span class="hljs-string">response.url();</span>
      <span class="hljs-string">if</span> <span class="hljs-string">(url.includes(''/rest/v1/tags''))</span> {
        <span class="hljs-string">const</span> <span class="hljs-string">existingRequest</span> <span class="hljs-string">=</span> <span class="hljs-string">apiRequests.find((req)</span> <span class="hljs-string">=&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-string">req.url</span> <span class="hljs-string">===</span> <span class="hljs-string">url);</span>
        <span class="hljs-string">if</span> <span class="hljs-string">(existingRequest)</span> {
          <span class="hljs-string">existingRequest.status</span> <span class="hljs-string">=</span> <span class="hljs-string">response.status();</span>
        }
      }
    }<span class="hljs-string">);</span>

    <span class="hljs-string">await</span> <span class="hljs-string">page.goto(''/''</span>, { <span class="hljs-attr">waitUntil:</span> <span class="hljs-string">''networkidle''</span> }<span class="hljs-string">);</span>
    <span class="hljs-string">await</span> <span class="hljs-string">acceptCookies(page);</span>
    <span class="hljs-string">await</span> <span class="hljs-string">waitForAngularToLoad(page</span>, <span class="hljs-number">500</span><span class="hljs-string">);</span>
    <span class="hljs-string">await</span> <span class="hljs-string">page.waitForSelector(''</span>[<span class="hljs-string">data-testid="tags-container"</span>]<span class="hljs-string">'', {
      timeout: 500,
    });

    try {
      await waitForApiCall(page, ''</span><span class="hljs-string">/rest/v1/tags''</span>, <span class="hljs-number">300</span><span class="hljs-string">);</span>
    } <span class="hljs-string">catch</span> <span class="hljs-string">(error)</span> {
      <span class="hljs-string">//</span> <span class="hljs-string">Continue</span> <span class="hljs-string">test</span> <span class="hljs-string">even</span> <span class="hljs-string">if</span> <span class="hljs-string">API</span> <span class="hljs-string">call</span> <span class="hljs-string">detection</span> <span class="hljs-string">fails</span>
    }

    <span class="hljs-string">await</span> <span class="hljs-string">page.waitForTimeout(1000);</span>
    <span class="hljs-string">await</span> <span class="hljs-string">page.waitForSelector(''</span>[<span class="hljs-string">data-testid="tag-item"</span>]<span class="hljs-string">'', { timeout: 500 });

    const tagsContainer = page.locator(''</span>[<span class="hljs-string">data-testid="tags-container"</span>]<span class="hljs-string">'');
    await expect(tagsContainer).toBeVisible();

    const expectedTags = [
      { name: ''</span><span class="hljs-string">Angular''</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">color:</span> <span class="hljs-string">''#DD0031''</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">icon:</span> <span class="hljs-string">''angular.svg''</span> }<span class="hljs-string">,</span>
      { <span class="hljs-attr">name:</span> <span class="hljs-string">''TypeScript''</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">color:</span> <span class="hljs-string">''#007ACC''</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">icon:</span> <span class="hljs-string">''typescript.svg''</span> }<span class="hljs-string">,</span>
      { <span class="hljs-attr">name:</span> <span class="hljs-string">''JavaScript''</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">color:</span> <span class="hljs-string">''#F7DF1E''</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">icon:</span> <span class="hljs-string">''javascript.svg''</span> }<span class="hljs-string">,</span>
    <span class="hljs-string">];</span>

    <span class="hljs-string">for</span> <span class="hljs-string">(const</span> <span class="hljs-string">tag</span> <span class="hljs-string">of</span> <span class="hljs-string">expectedTags)</span> {
      <span class="hljs-string">const</span> <span class="hljs-string">tagItem</span> <span class="hljs-string">=</span> <span class="hljs-string">page.locator(</span>
        <span class="hljs-string">`</span>[<span class="hljs-string">data-testid="tag-item"</span>][<span class="hljs-string">data-tag-name="$</span>{<span class="hljs-string">tag.name</span>}<span class="hljs-string">"]`,
      );
      await expect(tagItem).toBeVisible();

      const tagName = tagItem.locator(''[data-testid="</span><span class="hljs-string">tag-name"</span>]<span class="hljs-string">'');
      await expect(tagName).toHaveText(tag.name);
    }

    expect(apiRequests.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
  });
});

</span></code></pre><h2>Configure GitHub Action</h2><div>I spent a few minutes on this one; I bet about 3 hours. There was an annoying issue with</div><pre data-language="plain"><code class="javascript">@rollup
</code></pre><div>The whole CI step took about 8 minutes. There also was an issue with timeouts when waiting for the Supabase instance.</div><pre data-language="javascript"><code class="yaml"><span class="hljs-attr">name:</span> <span class="hljs-string">E2E</span> <span class="hljs-string">Tests</span> <span class="hljs-string">with</span> <span class="hljs-string">Local</span> <span class="hljs-string">Supabase</span>

<span class="hljs-attr">on:</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">push:</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">branches:</span> [ <span class="hljs-string">main</span>, <span class="hljs-string">develop</span> ]
  <span class="hljs-attr">pull_request:</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">branches:</span> [ <span class="hljs-string">main</span> ]

<span class="hljs-attr">jobs:</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">e2e:</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">if:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"!contains(github.event.head_commit.message, ''E2E'')"</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">runs-on:</span> <span class="hljs-string">ubuntu-latest</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">concurrency:</span>
      <span class="hljs-attr">group:</span> <span class="hljs-string">${{</span> <span class="hljs-string">github.workflow</span> <span class="hljs-string">}}-${{</span> <span class="hljs-string">github.ref</span> <span class="hljs-string">}}</span>
      <span class="hljs-attr">cancel-in-progress:</span> <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>

    <span class="hljs-attr">steps:</span>
      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">uses:</span> <span class="hljs-string">actions/checkout@v4</span>
      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">uses:</span> <span class="hljs-string">actions/setup-node@v4</span>
        <span class="hljs-attr">with:</span> { <span class="hljs-attr">node-version:</span> <span class="hljs-number">20</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">cache:</span> <span class="hljs-string">npm</span> }
      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">run:</span> <span class="hljs-string">npm</span> <span class="hljs-string">ci</span>
      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">run:</span> <span class="hljs-string">|
          node - &lt;&lt;''NODE''
          const v = require(''./package.json'').devDependencies.rollup ?? ''latest'';
          console.log(''ℹ Installing native Rollup helper for'', v);
          NODE
</span>      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">run:</span> <span class="hljs-string">npm</span> <span class="hljs-string">install</span> <span class="hljs-string">--no-save</span> <span class="hljs-string">@rollup/rollup-linux-x64-gnu@$(node</span> <span class="hljs-string">-p</span> <span class="hljs-string">"require(''./package.json'').devDependencies.rollup || ''4''"</span><span class="hljs-string">)</span>

      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">uses:</span> <span class="hljs-string">supabase/setup-cli@v1</span>
        <span class="hljs-attr">with:</span> { <span class="hljs-attr">version:</span> <span class="hljs-number">2.24</span><span class="hljs-number">.3</span> }

      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">name:</span> <span class="hljs-string">Start</span> <span class="hljs-string">Supabase</span>
        <span class="hljs-attr">env:</span> { <span class="hljs-attr">SUPABASE_TELEMETRY_DISABLED:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"1"</span> }
        <span class="hljs-attr">run:</span> <span class="hljs-string">supabase</span> <span class="hljs-string">start</span> <span class="hljs-string">&amp;</span>

      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">name:</span> <span class="hljs-string">Wait</span> <span class="hljs-string">for</span> <span class="hljs-string">Supabase</span> <span class="hljs-string">(≤180</span> <span class="hljs-string">s)</span>
        <span class="hljs-attr">run:</span> <span class="hljs-string">npx</span> <span class="hljs-string">--yes</span> <span class="hljs-string">wait-on</span> <span class="hljs-string">tcp:127.0.0.1:54321</span> <span class="hljs-string">tcp:127.0.0.1:54322</span> <span class="hljs-string">--timeout</span> <span class="hljs-number">180000</span>

      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">run:</span> <span class="hljs-string">echo</span> <span class="hljs-string">"SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321"</span> <span class="hljs-string">&gt;&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-string">$GITHUB_ENV</span>
      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">run:</span> <span class="hljs-string">|
          echo "SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=$(supabase status -o env | grep SUPABASE_ANON_KEY | cut -d= -f2)" &gt;&gt; $GITHUB_ENV
</span>
      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">run:</span> <span class="hljs-string">npx</span> <span class="hljs-string">playwright</span> <span class="hljs-string">install</span> <span class="hljs-string">--with-deps</span>
      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">run:</span> <span class="hljs-string">npm</span> <span class="hljs-string">run</span> <span class="hljs-string">e2e:local</span>
        <span class="hljs-attr">env:</span> { <span class="hljs-attr">CI:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"true"</span> }

      <span class="hljs-bullet">-</span> <span class="hljs-attr">uses:</span> <span class="hljs-string">actions/upload-artifact@v4</span>
        <span class="hljs-attr">if:</span> <span class="hljs-string">always()</span>
        <span class="hljs-attr">with:</span> { <span class="hljs-attr">name:</span> <span class="hljs-string">playwright-report</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">path:</span> <span class="hljs-string">playwright-report</span> }

</code></pre><div>GitHub Actions also has a cool feature: run only if the commit message includes the specified commit message. I''d rather run E2E tests locally.</div><pre data-language="plain"><code class="javascript"><span class="hljs-attr">if</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"!contains(github.event.head_commit.message, ''E2E'')"</span>
</code></pre><div>That''s all! I''m happy that it was fast. It took me only a few days xD. But the most time I spent with the Supabase locally and seeds.</div><div>Thank you all!</div>', false, '2025-09-17T00:00:00+00:00', 'https://aqdbdmepncxxuanlymwr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/covers/ChatGPT%20Image%2011%20sie%202026,%2020_53_29.png', '[]'::jsonb),
  ('ad911e2f-1677-4dda-abd9-d159a9ac6538', 'a3b3cf93-6956-41c7-bec0-13f95dd42f26', 'Angular SSR/SSG App with Supabse - Auth, Guards, API calls Part 1/2(?)', 'A deep dive into refactoring Supabase auth in an Angular SSR/SSG app. Covers application layering (infrastructure, state, domain), provider-agnostic auth design, async guards with CanMatchFn, and why blocking app bootstrap isn''t always a bug. Explains how pendingUntilEvent finally makes the native Supabase client work with server-side rendering, and walks through a testing strategy with Playwright''s setup', '<h2 id="hello-again!">Hello again!</h2><div>Maybe you remember, there were issues with the Supabase client, also with SSR / SSG data fetch for pre-rendering. Also some issues with guards, auth, app freeze etc.</div><div>The biggest issue is the Supabase <code>createClient()</code> method — it doesn''t return anything, it''s just fire and forget. Unlike for example Keycloak, where <code>createClient</code> is Promise-based.</div><div>Current code was autogenerated by CC, and all the logic is stored inside a SignalStore — WTF XD.</div><div>Yeah, this is a very big mistake.</div><div>A well-designed application should be split into layers, and we mixed 3 layers:</div><ul><li><strong>Infrastructure layer</strong> — it''s a kind of bridge to our Domain layer. Here we have SDKs like Supabase, or even Router from Angular. This should be separated from our domain, because if the Supabase SDK leaks into our app, it becomes a 3rd-party dependency — harder to switch providers, harder to test because dependencies are harder to mock.</li><li><strong>State layer</strong> — our SignalStore, keeps application state, keeps communicating with infra, but doesn''t implement logic. It also shouldn''t depend on infra directly — better to use wrappers, and I''ll show how and why.</li><li><strong>Domain layer</strong> — our app''s business logic. Should be framework-agnostic (hard to achieve) but most importantly should be independent from infra. Want to switch from Supabase to Keycloak? Want to change from Supabase API to your own .NET API? No problem — we just swap our bridge to infra, the app stays the same and behaves the same. This is key. But to be honest, it''s not that important in some gigantic corpo apps — changing infra would never happen there. But in apps like this one, it could. Supabase is not free, there are cheaper alternatives, and prices can change.</li></ul><div>When I was trying to fix my app — and by "fix" I mean write better code than CC — I faced a few issues. Let''s deep-dive into the first one: a Guard for the Admin app.</div><div>We need to block all traffic inside the app for non-Admin users. There''s a little issue though — the session is initialized under the hood. We can await the session, manually of course. I asked Claude, Gemini, Codex and probably other LLMs for help. Most common scenarios, there were basically two:</div><ul><li><code>await getSession()</code> — in the guard, LOL XD</li><li><code>authStore.init()</code> — in the app initializer. This one was proposed 99 times.</li></ul><div>So, what''s bad about both of them?</div><div>The first one, <code>await getSession()</code> — basically, it''s fine. For me, if it were a corpo app, I''d do that, but in the app initializer, just to block app bootstrap. Quick win. But not so elegant for me. Why not in a guard? Because we will be 3rd party agnostic. Also, every guard would need to implement it, right? A lot of code duplication, and infra leaks into our domain. What if we want to switch from Supabase? Refactor. We <code>await</code> for the session, sure — but what if another provider doesn''t expose a session, or it''s not a Promise? We''d need to write a mapper/wrapper. Extra logic to maintain.</div><div>The second one — SignalStore exposes hook methods, <code>onInit</code>, <code>onDestroy</code>, but they only work with components, because I think SignalStore is component-based. I saw tutorials with a mix of classic NgRx store and SignalStore in one app. There''s also an open issue that SignalStore should implement something like a constructor. I bet SignalStore is designed this way on purpose, to block users from exactly the ideas the LLMs keep proposing. The chats of course pushed this as the best-ever solution, the one and only — but when I asked them to actually implement what they proposed, they couldn''t. Others think SignalStore is for state, for f*ck''s sake, not for app init — it''s not created to perform that kind of logic. It was created to enforce clean app layer separation, not All-in-One.</div><h2 class="ql-align-center" id="supabase-made-the-app-freeze">Supabase made the app freeze</h2><div>As you remember, I had an issue with Supabase — it was freezing the app with SSG or SSR on. It''s because we were using <code>createClient()</code>, not <code>createServerClient()</code> from <code>@supabase/ssr</code>. Supabase keeps the session in local storage, and server-side we don''t have it — we need to transfer the session from the server via cookies, and from the client we also need to send cookies to the server to perform authorized calls.</div><div>I made a quick fix (took a long time to figure out) with <code>runOutsideAngular</code>. It helped — I moved to PostgREST API calls, so I wasn''t using <code>supabaseClient</code> on the server side at all. It took me a little time to realize this. I thought about how to change it, and the fix was surprisingly easy — just don''t create the client on the server side.</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">createSupabaseClient</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>): <span class="hljs-title class_">SupabaseClient</span> | <span class="hljs-literal">null</span> {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> config = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-variable constant_">SUPABASE_CONFIG</span>);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> platform = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-variable constant_">PLATFORM_ID</span>);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> zone = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">NgZone</span>);

  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">isPlatformServer</span>(platform)
    ? <span class="hljs-literal">null</span>
    : <span class="hljs-title function_">createClient</span>(config.<span class="hljs-property">supabaseUrl</span>, config.<span class="hljs-property">supabaseKey</span>);
}
</code></pre><div>And the issue is gone — Supabase no longer freezes the app.</div><h2 class="ql-align-center" id="my-way-to-handle-supabase-auth">My way to handle Supabase Auth</h2><div>I wouldn''t say this is the best way — I''d say it''s good. Bad code is code that doesn''t work; the worst code is code that works but no one knows why. We live in a world of trade-offs.</div><div>I spent a lot of time thinking about how to handle auth. How to do it right.</div><div>As I mentioned, in a commercial app I would use <code>await getSession()</code> in the app initializer — so I''d block the whole app until the Supabase session is ready. Then we have access to the user and their roles, everything we need to decide how to manage people trying to access our restricted app. On the blog side, we can mostly skip it — most of the time, the session will initialize under the hood before we even need to make an API call to access user-specific data. Sometimes (I bet almost never) it might surface some <code>getProfile</code> issue, but there''s no app without bugs. A 1h fix that makes the app work almost perfectly fine vs a 30h fix that covers 0.01% of edge cases. I''m choosing the 30h path — why? Just because I like it. I want to try to create the best solution. I dived into rabbit holes so many times during this fix. Sometimes I got a solution that broke another part of the app. Best from the point of view of programming as an Art (I''m not saying I achieved it XD), worst from a business point of view.</div><div>I feel I''ll move away from Supabase — it''s quite expensive for this blog. So I need to be aware about every dependency I use.</div><h3 class="ql-align-center" id="create-client">Create Client</h3><div>In the actual codebase, the client is created in <code>appInitializer</code> via a service constructor. So the service is the kickstarter for our app — but it handles way too much: creating the client, handling auth changes, handling session changes. In most cases of injecting <code>SupabaseService</code>, we only need the client itself.</div><div>Here one of the best things about Angular kicks in — DI. It''s really underused. I don''t see a lot of injection tokens around; most people use DI only to inject services, that''s all. This is a powerful pattern, because we depend on abstraction, not on the actual implementation. So we can decouple parts of our application — why does it matter? For the sake of testing — not only unit testing or e2e, but also A/B testing. We can also provide a different client for different routes:</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript">app.<span class="hljs-property">routes</span>.<span class="hljs-property">ts</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> { <span class="hljs-title class_">Routes</span> } <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">''@angular/router''</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> {
  <span class="hljs-variable constant_">SUPABASE_CONFIG</span>,
  <span class="hljs-variable constant_">SUPABASE_CLIENT</span>,
  <span class="hljs-title class_">SupabaseService</span>,
  createSupabaseClient,
} <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">''@shared/core/supabase''</span>;

<span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-attr">routes</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">Routes</span> = [
  {
    <span class="hljs-attr">path</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''''</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">loadChildren</span>: <span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">import</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''./features/main-page/main-page.routes''</span>),
  },
  {
    <span class="hljs-attr">path</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''post''</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">providers</span>: [
      {
        <span class="hljs-attr">provide</span>: <span class="hljs-variable constant_">SUPABASE_CONFIG</span>,
        <span class="hljs-attr">useValue</span>: {
          <span class="hljs-attr">supabaseUrl</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''https://other-project.supabase.co''</span>,
          <span class="hljs-attr">supabaseKey</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''public-anon-key-for-that-project''</span>,
        },
      },
      {
        <span class="hljs-attr">provide</span>: <span class="hljs-variable constant_">SUPABASE_CLIENT</span>,
        <span class="hljs-attr">useFactory</span>: createSupabaseClient,
      },
      <span class="hljs-title class_">SupabaseService</span>,
    ],
    <span class="hljs-attr">loadChildren</span>: <span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">import</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''./features/post/post.routes''</span>),
  },
  {
    <span class="hljs-attr">path</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''**''</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">redirectTo</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''''</span>,
  },
];

</code></pre><div>And BANG — we have a connection to a different client. Want to ship a new feature, but you''re not sure if it''s worth it? Could it lead to a big payment for some 3rd-party service? Use another client with limitations. Everything goes fine? Just provide the main service back, without touching any other code. Want to test specific scenarios? Create your own implementation of the client, test the real data flow through services, and mock the 3rd-party library.</div><div>The actual implementation:</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">provideCore</span>(<span class="hljs-params">{ routes }: <span class="hljs-title class_">CoreOptions</span></span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> [
    <span class="hljs-title function_">provideBrowserGlobalErrorListeners</span>(),
    <span class="hljs-title function_">provideHttpClient</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">withFetch</span>()),
    <span class="hljs-title function_">provideZoneChangeDetection</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">eventCoalescing</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">true</span> }),
    <span class="hljs-title function_">provideRouter</span>(routes, <span class="hljs-title function_">withComponentInputBinding</span>()),
    <span class="hljs-title function_">provideClientHydration</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">withEventReplay</span>()),
    {
      <span class="hljs-attr">provide</span>: <span class="hljs-variable constant_">SUPABASE_CONFIG</span>,
      <span class="hljs-attr">useValue</span>: {
        <span class="hljs-attr">supabaseUrl</span>: environment.<span class="hljs-property">supabaseUrl</span>,
        <span class="hljs-attr">supabaseKey</span>: environment.<span class="hljs-property">supabaseKey</span>,
      },
    },
    {
      <span class="hljs-attr">provide</span>: <span class="hljs-variable constant_">SUPABASE_CLIENT</span>,
      <span class="hljs-attr">useFactory</span>: createSupabaseClient,
    },
    <span class="hljs-title function_">provideAppInitializer</span>(supabaseInitializer),
  ];
}
<span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-variable constant_">SUPABASE_CLIENT</span> = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">InjectionToken</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title class_">SupabaseClient</span>&gt;(<span class="hljs-string">''SupabaseClient''</span>);

<span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">createSupabaseClient</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>): <span class="hljs-title class_">SupabaseClient</span> {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> config = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-variable constant_">SUPABASE_CONFIG</span>);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> platform = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-variable constant_">PLATFORM_ID</span>);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> zone = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">NgZone</span>);

  <span class="hljs-comment">//<span class="hljs-doctag">TODO:</span> Implement server-side authentication logic with SSR and cookies/headers</span>
  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">isPlatformServer</span>(platform)
    ? zone.<span class="hljs-title function_">runOutsideAngular</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">createClient</span>(config.<span class="hljs-property">supabaseUrl</span>, config.<span class="hljs-property">supabaseKey</span>))
    : <span class="hljs-title function_">createClient</span>(config.<span class="hljs-property">supabaseUrl</span>, config.<span class="hljs-property">supabaseKey</span>);
}
</code></pre><div>As you can see, I''m creating the client on the server side too — that''s because I forced the Supabase native client to fetch data for SSR/SSG. But I''ll talk about that later.</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">readonly</span> client = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-variable constant_">SUPABASE_CLIENT</span>);
</code></pre><div>And whole app stays the same, no changes are needed, this os the power of DI.</div><h3 class="ql-align-center" id="initialise-auth">Initialise Auth</h3><div>As you saw before in <code>core.ts</code>, there is:</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">provideAppInitializer</span>(supabaseInitializer),
</code></pre><div>Maybe let''s rename it:</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">provideAppInitializer</span>(authInitializer),
</code></pre><div>Looks better — Supabase is already initialized with the client. The initializer method is just a tiny wrapper for the service:</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">authInitializer</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>): <span class="hljs-built_in">void</span> {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> supabaseService = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">SupabaseService</span>);
  supabaseService.<span class="hljs-title function_">initializeAuth</span>()
}
</code></pre><div>I read the docs:</div><blockquote><span style="color: rgb(82, 82, 82); background-color: rgb(252, 252, 252);">The session returned can be null if the session is not detected which can happen in the event a user is not signed-in or has logged out.</span></blockquote><div>That''s perfect. I made a tests, and the session returns <code>null</code> even if the client has some errors like a wrong API key etc. It provides a more bulletproof implementation (errors-wise, I mean). We can rely on the session.</div><div class="ql-align-center"><img src="data:image/png;base64,"></div><div>What docs says? We should take care on the server:</div><blockquote>const { data: { user } } = await supabase.auth.getUser()</blockquote><blockquote>Should always be used when checking for user authorization on the server. On the client, you can instead use getSession().session.user for faster results. getSession is insecure on the server.</blockquote><blockquote>IMPORTANT SECURITY NOTICE: If using an insecure storage medium, such as cookies or request headers, the user object returned by this function must not be trusted. Always verify the JWT using getClaims() or your own JWT verification library to securely establish the user''s identity and access. You can also use getUser() to fetch the user object directly from the Auth server for this purpose.</blockquote><div>Why is it more important on the server? To be honest — right now, someone could easily change their role to admin and access the admin panel (only with change role in the data stored in localStorage by using Dev tools). But this is always possible; you can''t prevent the FE from being inspected. I mean, you could keep JS code on the BE, secure it by JWT, and send it from BE, but no point struggling — we have RLS to block API calls. Second, the code is on GitHub in a public repo xD</div><div>Maybe you remember that LLMs proposed <code>await getSession()</code> in guards, to wait for the session to be initialized. Yeah, nice, but:</div><blockquote><span style="color: rgb(82, 82, 82); background-color: rgb(252, 252, 252);">It''s best practice and highly recommended to extract the access token (JWT) and store it in memory for further use in your application.</span></blockquote><blockquote class="ql-indent-1"><span style="color: rgb(82, 82, 82); background-color: rgb(252, 252, 252);">Avoid frequent calls to </span><code style="color: rgb(23, 23, 23); background-color: rgb(243, 243, 243);">supabase.auth.getSession()</code><span style="color: rgb(82, 82, 82); background-color: rgb(252, 252, 252);"> for the same purpose.</span></blockquote><div>Let''s create a little benchmark. The first <code>getSession()</code> call takes some time, the rest — not really.</div><div class="ql-indent-1"><img src="data:image/png;base64,"></div><div>But:</div><blockquote><em>this function is synchronized across all tabs using the LockManager API</em></blockquote><div>When I opened a few tabs, the time increased — even on runs other than the first.</div><div>But it''s not only about micro performance optimizations — it''s about how apps are built. We can extract the token and pass it through the app. We operate on the JWT, which is common across auth providers, so we decouple the app. Business logic knows the JWT and the JWT data, app bussiness logic doesn''t care about the provider. .NET, Auth0, Keycloack, Azure? JWT is JWT</div><h3 class="ql-align-center" id="extract-data-from-supabase">Extract data from Supabase</h3><div>The docs say to extract the JWT. Here''s how it looks:</div><pre data-language="xml"><code class="hljs language-xml">{
  "data": {
    "session": {
      "access_token": "<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">ACCESS_TOKEN</span>&gt;</span>",
      "token_type": "bearer",
      "expires_in": 3600,
      "expires_at": 1700000000,
      "refresh_token": "<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">REFRESH_TOKEN</span>&gt;</span>",
      "user": {
        "id": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111",
        "aud": "authenticated",
        "role": "authenticated",
        "email": "example@email.com",
        "email_confirmed_at": "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z",
        "phone": "",
        "last_sign_in_at": "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z",
        "app_metadata": {
          "provider": "email",
          "providers": [
            "email"
          ]
        },
        "user_metadata": {
          "email": "example@email.com",
          "email_verified": false,
          "phone_verified": false,
          "sub": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111"
        },
        "identities": [
          {
            "identity_id": "22222222-2222-2222-2222-222222222222",
            "id": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111",
            "user_id": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111",
            "identity_data": {
              "email": "example@email.com",
              "email_verified": false,
              "phone_verified": false,
              "sub": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111"
            },
            "provider": "email",
            "last_sign_in_at": "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z",
            "created_at": "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z",
            "updated_at": "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z",
            "email": "example@email.com"
          }
        ],
        "created_at": "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z",
        "updated_at": "2024-01-01T00:00:00Z",
        "is_anonymous": false
      }
    }
  },
  "error": null
}
</code></pre><div>All this data isn''t necessary right now. Remember about YAGNI — <em>You Aren''t Gonna Need It</em>.</div><div>We need the role — but not this Supabase role, our own from <code>user_metadata</code>— and the user <code>id</code>. Both are stored in the User object. Basic type of User don''t include a metadata so we need to extend user to <code>UserWithRole</code></div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">UserService</span> {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">readonly</span> appUser = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">BehaviorSubject</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title class_">UserWithRole</span> | <span class="hljs-literal">null</span> | <span class="hljs-literal">undefined</span>&gt;(<span class="hljs-literal">undefined</span>);

  <span class="hljs-keyword">get</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">appUser$</span>(): <span class="hljs-title class_">Observable</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title class_">UserWithRole</span> | <span class="hljs-literal">null</span>&gt; {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">appUser</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">asObservable</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">pipe</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">filter</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">user</span> =&gt;</span> user !== <span class="hljs-literal">undefined</span>));
  }

  <span class="hljs-title function_">setAppUser</span>(<span class="hljs-attr">user</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">UserWithRole</span> | <span class="hljs-literal">null</span>): <span class="hljs-built_in">void</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">appUser</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">next</span>(user);
  }
}
</code></pre><div>Later on I''ll provide a fancier way to handle this. For now, we''re pushing some kind of <code>User</code> based on the <code>User</code> from Supabase. It''s not great — we need to have a mapper, ideally a generic one, since we only need <code>id</code> and a <code>Role</code> array. That should be easy.</div><div>This way, we have auth data as a store, not depending on the provider. It can be used with other providers and will populate the same data into our app, independent of who provides it. <code>appUser$</code> can be easily mocked in tests, in any chain we want — want a unit test with a user? Guards? Components with routing, not in isolation? Different cases? Different roles? All can now be done just by mocking <code>appUser$</code> — NOT the whole Supabase auth flow.</div><div>Let''s break it down into smaller pieces:</div><div><strong>Why </strong><code><strong>BehaviorSubject</strong></code><strong>?</strong> We need an initial value, and we need an immediate value emitted for new subscribers the moment they subscribe — no waiting for the next emission, no missing the current state.</div><div><strong>Why </strong><code><strong>null | User | undefined</strong></code><strong>?</strong> This maps directly to the Supabase auth states:</div><ul><li><code>undefined</code> — auth hasn''t initialized yet, we don''t know anything about the user. This is the initial <code>BehaviorSubject</code> value. We filter it out from the public stream, so subscribers never see the "I don''t know yet" state — they wait until we actually know something.</li><li><code>null</code> — auth is initialized, and there''s no logged-in user. The session check came back empty. This is a known state, and subscribers should react to it (e.g. guards redirect to login).</li><li><code>UserWithRole</code> — auth is initialized, and we have a logged-in user with their role mapped.</li><li></li></ul><div>So <code>undefined</code> means "not ready", <code>null</code> means "ready, but anonymous", and <code>UserWithRole</code> means "ready and authenticated". The <code>filter(user =&gt; user !== undefined)</code> makes sure consumers only see the two ready states, never the limbo.</div><h3 class="ql-align-center" id="async-admin-guard">Async Admin Guard</h3><div>As I mentioned, LLMs tried to convince me that blocking app bootstrap is a bug. I shouldn''t have said that — I think both ways are correct. And even if we need to block the entire app based on the user, is there any point in handling errors gracefully? If there''s an auth error, there''s no app. Simple as that. This is mostly used for admin panels etc., and the only thing that matters there is UX. Sometimes UX is considered a secondary concern. Sometimes it''s not considered at all.</div><div>There''s no single correct approach. It''s all a matter of design choices. Blocking app bootstrap is the easier path.</div><div>We chose the harder way (because it''s fun):</div><div>ts</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-attr">authAdminGuard</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">CanMatchFn</span> = (): <span class="hljs-title class_">Observable</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">boolean</span> | <span class="hljs-title class_">UrlTree</span>&gt; =&gt; {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> userService = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">UserService</span>);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> router = <span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">Router</span>);

  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> userService.<span class="hljs-property">appUser$</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">pipe</span>(
    <span class="hljs-title function_">take</span>(<span class="hljs-number">1</span>),
    <span class="hljs-title function_">map</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">user</span> =&gt;</span> {
      <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (user?.<span class="hljs-property">app_metadata</span>.<span class="hljs-property">role</span> === <span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span>.<span class="hljs-property">ADMIN</span>) {
        <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>;
      }
      <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> router.<span class="hljs-title function_">createUrlTree</span>([<span class="hljs-string">''/login''</span>]);
    })
  );
};
</code></pre><div>The new cool async guard function can return an observable. We''re subscribing to <code>appUser$</code>. We filtered out <code>undefined</code>, so the guard will wait for auth to resolve — and as you remember, the session check is nicely error-proof and quite fast, so we can''t jump into limbo. We''re waiting for the first signal (to be honest, the second one after <code>undefined</code> ;)). We use <code>take(1)</code>, because we only care about the first shot, then we unsubscribe</div><h3 class="ql-align-center" id="handling-userprofile">Handling UserProfile</h3><div>Based on that we can create a simple store to handle UserProfile</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"> <span class="hljs-title function_">withMethods</span>(
    <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">store, profileService = inject(ProfileService)</span>) =&gt;</span> ({
      <span class="hljs-attr">loadProfile</span>: rxMethod&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> | <span class="hljs-literal">null</span>&gt;(
        <span class="hljs-title function_">pipe</span>(
          <span class="hljs-title function_">distinctUntilChanged</span>(),
          <span class="hljs-title function_">tap</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">patchState</span>(store, { <span class="hljs-attr">loading</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">error</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">null</span> })),
          <span class="hljs-title function_">switchMap</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">id</span> =&gt;</span> {
            <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (!id) {
              <span class="hljs-title function_">patchState</span>(store, { <span class="hljs-attr">userProfile</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">null</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">loading</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">false</span> });
              <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">of</span>(<span class="hljs-literal">null</span>);
            }
            <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> profileService.<span class="hljs-title function_">getProfile</span>(id).<span class="hljs-title function_">pipe</span>(
              <span class="hljs-title function_">tapResponse</span>({
                <span class="hljs-attr">next</span>: <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">profile</span> =&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">patchState</span>(store, { <span class="hljs-attr">userProfile</span>: profile, <span class="hljs-attr">loading</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">false</span> }),
                <span class="hljs-attr">error</span>: <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-attr">err</span>: <span class="hljs-built_in">unknown</span></span>) =&gt;</span>
                  <span class="hljs-title function_">patchState</span>(store, {
                    <span class="hljs-attr">userProfile</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">null</span>,
                    <span class="hljs-attr">loading</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">false</span>,
                    <span class="hljs-attr">error</span>: <span class="hljs-string">`Failed to fetch profile: <span class="hljs-subst">${err <span class="hljs-keyword">instanceof</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">Error</span> ? err.message : <span class="hljs-built_in">String</span>(err)}</span>`</span>,
                  }),
              })
            );
          })
        )
      ),
    })
  ),
  <span class="hljs-title function_">withHooks</span>({
    <span class="hljs-title function_">onInit</span>(<span class="hljs-params">store, userService = inject(UserService)</span>) {
      store.<span class="hljs-title function_">loadProfile</span>(userService.<span class="hljs-property">appUser$</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">pipe</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">map</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">u</span> =&gt;</span> u?.<span class="hljs-property">id</span> ?? <span class="hljs-literal">null</span>)));
    },
  })
</code></pre><div>This is a quick code snippet. I''ll refactor all stores at once later, but as you can see, we can rely on the observable and the store will automatically update data when <code>user.id</code> changes. If we want to test our component in isolation, we can mock the store — but that can be harder to achieve. A quick way (or maybe even a better way) is to use the actual store with the component and just mock <code>appUser$</code>.</div><div>Now, when we refresh the page with a user logged in, we have this awful flicker in the navbar — the <em>Sign In</em> button changes to <em>Sign Out</em> and user data becomes visible. Yeah, that''s because we didn''t create a proper server-side Supabase client, and we didn''t push the session or JWT to the server, so we can''t do an API call on the server side. I''ll try to handle this in the next part.</div><div>I really wanted this front page to be SSG. In React there''s something like server components, if I remember correctly — so part of a page can be SSG and some components can be SSR, which would fit this case. Unfortunately, Angular and AnalogJS don''t support this.</div><h3 class="ql-align-center" id="bonus:-forcing-the-supabase-client-to-fetch-data-for-server-side-generation">Bonus: Forcing the Supabase client to fetch data for server-side generation</h3><div>This is a little bonus. Maybe you remember I had issues with data fetched by the native Supabase client. It wasn''t working — Angular just ignored Supabase methods and didn''t wait for the data fetch. I had some time now, did a lot of refactoring, and configured Supabase auth in a better way.</div><div>Maybe you remember I used PostgREST (built into Supabase) to handle endpoints with the native HTTP client. It works fine, because it''s native Angular and Angular knows it''s important, it''s synchronised with pending task.</div><div>All my data is now propagated as Observables instead of Promises, which are native in the Supabase client. (I don''t like Promises — they''re broken too often.) My store consumes Observables. I think this is good. I could consume Promises, but Observables are more the Angular way of programming. And luckily, this helped me a lot with Supabase.</div><div>Because Angular added in v20 a new <code>rxjs-interop</code> operator:</div><blockquote><span style="color: oklch(0.1937 0.006 300.98); background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">pendingUntilEvent</span></blockquote><blockquote><span style="color: oklch(0.1937 0.006 300.98); background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">Operator which makes the application unstable until the observable emits, completes, errors, or is unsubscribed.</span></blockquote><blockquote><span style="color: oklch(0.1937 0.006 300.98); background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">Operator which makes the application unstable until the observable emits, completes, errors, or is unsubscribed.</span></blockquote><blockquote><span style="color: oklch(0.1937 0.006 300.98); background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">Use this operator in observables whose subscriptions are important for rendering and should be included in SSR serialization.</span></blockquote><div>https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/main/packages/core/rxjs-interop/src/pending_until_event.ts#L21-L64</div><div>Under the hood, it just calls <code>taskService.add()</code>. So we don''t really need RxJS for this — I think we could wrap Supabase Promises and handle the wait the same way. But I like this approach. I didn''t even know it existed — the docs were super useful in that part of SSR.</div><div>Code:</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">getPosts</span>(): <span class="hljs-title class_">Observable</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title class_">Post</span>[]&gt; {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">from</span>(
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">this</span>.<span class="hljs-property">client</span>
      .<span class="hljs-title function_">from</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''posts''</span>)
      .<span class="hljs-title function_">select</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''*, author:profiles(id,username,avatar_url), post_tags(tags(id,name,color,icon))''</span>)
      .<span class="hljs-title function_">eq</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''is_draft''</span>, <span class="hljs-literal">false</span>)
      .<span class="hljs-title function_">order</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''created_at''</span>, { <span class="hljs-attr">ascending</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">false</span> })
  ).<span class="hljs-title function_">pipe</span>(
    <span class="hljs-title function_">map</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">x</span> =&gt;</span> (x.<span class="hljs-property">error</span> ? [] : x.<span class="hljs-property">data</span>)),
    <span class="hljs-title function_">pendingUntilEvent</span>()
  );
</code></pre><div>And we can use Supabase with SSR. I''ll definitely check if that is faster etc, but it''s nice, I like this.</div><div></div><h2 class="ql-align-center" id="tests">Tests</h2><div>We''ve covered a lot of architecture decisions — auth, layers, store, hydration. None of that matters if we can''t verify it works. Let''s talk about tests. I split them into two parts: unit tests (Karma + Jasmine, the Angular default) and e2e (Playwright).</div><h3 id="unit-tests">Unit tests</h3><div><strong>Guard test — </strong><code><strong>auth-admin.guard.spec.ts</strong></code></div><div>The guard is pure logic: take the first emission from <code>appUser$</code>, check role, return <code>true</code> or a <code>UrlTree</code>. Perfect for a unit test, because it has exactly one dependency (<code>UserService</code>) and we control its output completely.</div><div>ts</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">setup</span> = (<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-attr">appUser$</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">Observable</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title class_">UserWithRole</span> | <span class="hljs-literal">null</span>&gt;</span>) =&gt; {
  <span class="hljs-title class_">TestBed</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">configureTestingModule</span>({
    <span class="hljs-attr">providers</span>: [
      <span class="hljs-title function_">provideZonelessChangeDetection</span>(),
      <span class="hljs-title function_">provideRouter</span>([]),
      { <span class="hljs-attr">provide</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">UserService</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">useValue</span>: { appUser$ } },
    ],
  });

  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> router = <span class="hljs-title class_">TestBed</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">inject</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">Router</span>);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> createUrlTreeSpy = <span class="hljs-title function_">spyOn</span>(router, <span class="hljs-string">''createUrlTree''</span>).<span class="hljs-property">and</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">callThrough</span>();

  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">runGuard</span> = (<span class="hljs-params"></span>) =&gt;
    <span class="hljs-title class_">TestBed</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">runInInjectionContext</span>(
      <span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">authAdminGuard</span>({} <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Route</span>, []) <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Observable</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">boolean</span> | <span class="hljs-title class_">UrlTree</span>&gt;
    );

  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> { router, createUrlTreeSpy, runGuard };
};
</code></pre><div>A few things worth pointing out:</div><ul><li>We provide <code>UserService</code> with just <code>{ appUser$ }</code> — the guard doesn''t touch anything else. No need to mock methods we don''t call.</li><li><code>runInInjectionContext</code> is required because <code>authAdminGuard</code> uses <code>inject()</code> internally. Without it, you get <em>"NG0203: inject() must be called from an injection context"</em>.</li><li><code>provideRouter([])</code> gives us a real <code>Router</code> — we spy on <code>createUrlTree</code> to verify it''s called with the right args, but we don''t mock the whole router. Cheap and we get real behavior.</li></ul><div>Then the tests are straightforward — push different values into a <code>Subject</code> or <code>ReplaySubject</code>, assert the guard''s response:</div><div>ts</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">it</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''uses only the first emission from appUser$''</span>, <span class="hljs-title function_">async</span> () =&gt; {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> appUser$ = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Subject</span>&lt;<span class="hljs-title class_">UserWithRole</span> | <span class="hljs-literal">null</span>&gt;();
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> { createUrlTreeSpy, runGuard } = <span class="hljs-title function_">setup</span>(appUser$);

  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> result$ = <span class="hljs-title function_">firstValueFrom</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">runGuard</span>());
  appUser$.<span class="hljs-title function_">next</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">createUser</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span>.<span class="hljs-property">ADMIN</span>));
  appUser$.<span class="hljs-title function_">next</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">createUser</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">Roles</span>.<span class="hljs-property">READER</span>));
  appUser$.<span class="hljs-title function_">next</span>(<span class="hljs-literal">null</span>);

  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> result = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> result$;

  <span class="hljs-title function_">expect</span>(result).<span class="hljs-title function_">toBe</span>(<span class="hljs-literal">true</span>);
  <span class="hljs-title function_">expect</span>(createUrlTreeSpy).<span class="hljs-property">not</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">toHaveBeenCalled</span>();
});
</code></pre><div>This test verifies that <code>take(1)</code> actually does its job — push admin, then reader, then null, the guard should resolve with <code>true</code> and ignore the rest. If someone refactored <code>take(1)</code> away, this test catches it.</div><div><strong>Component test — </strong><code><strong>navbar.component.spec.ts</strong></code></div><div>This is where "mock the store vs use the real store" gets practical. I went with both approaches in one spec file — partly to show it''s possible, partly because they test different things.</div><div>The first describe block mocks <code>ProfileStore</code> directly:</div><div>ts</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript">profileStoreMock = {
  <span class="hljs-attr">userName</span>: signal&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> | <span class="hljs-literal">undefined</span>&gt;(<span class="hljs-literal">undefined</span>),
};
</code></pre><div>Quick, isolated, tests only the navbar''s reaction to a signal change. Good for verifying view logic.</div><div>The second describe block uses the <strong>real </strong><code><strong>ProfileStore</strong></code> and mocks only <code>UserService</code> and <code>ProfileService</code>:</div><div>ts</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> userServiceMock = {
  <span class="hljs-attr">appUser$</span>: <span class="hljs-title function_">of</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">id</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''user-1''</span> } <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">never</span>),
  <span class="hljs-attr">setAppUser</span>: jasmine.<span class="hljs-title function_">createSpy</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''setAppUser''</span>),
};
profileServiceMock = {
  <span class="hljs-attr">getProfile</span>: jasmine
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">createSpy</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''getProfile''</span>)
    .<span class="hljs-property">and</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">returnValue</span>(<span class="hljs-title function_">of</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">id</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''user-1''</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">username</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''Lukasz''</span> } <span class="hljs-keyword">as</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">never</span>)),
};
</code></pre><div>Now we test the <strong>full flow</strong>: <code>appUser$</code> emits → real store reacts → <code>getProfile</code> is called → real store updates → navbar renders. If anything breaks in that chain, this test catches it. Mock-store test wouldn''t.</div><div>Both have their place. Use what fits the question you''re asking.</div><h3 id="e2e-tests-(playwright)">E2E tests (Playwright)</h3><div>Unit tests verify pieces. E2E tests verify the whole thing actually works in a browser, against a server that responds, with real navigation. The challenge with auth-heavy apps is — how do you avoid logging in on every test?</div><div><strong>The setup project + </strong><code><strong>storageState</strong></code></div><div>Playwright has a concept of a <strong>setup project</strong> that runs once before the test suite and produces artifacts (like a saved storage state file) that all subsequent tests reuse. We have two: <code>auth-web.setup.ts</code> for a regular user, <code>auth-admin.setup.ts</code> for admin.</div><div>Here''s what <code>auth-admin.setup.ts</code> does:</div><div>ts</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-title function_">setup</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''authenticate admin via login flow''</span>, <span class="hljs-title function_">async</span> ({ page }) =&gt; {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">mockAuthenticatedUser</span>(page, {
    <span class="hljs-attr">id</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''admin-1''</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">email</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''admin@example.com''</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">username</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''Admin''</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">role</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''Admin''</span>,
  });

  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> page.<span class="hljs-title function_">goto</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''/''</span>);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> page.<span class="hljs-title function_">getByLabel</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''Email Address''</span>).<span class="hljs-title function_">fill</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''admin@example.com''</span>);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> page.<span class="hljs-title function_">getByLabel</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''Password''</span>).<span class="hljs-title function_">fill</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''admin123''</span>);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> page.<span class="hljs-title function_">getByLabel</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''Password''</span>).<span class="hljs-title function_">press</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''Enter''</span>);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">expect</span>(page.<span class="hljs-title function_">getByRole</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''button''</span>, { <span class="hljs-attr">name</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''Sign In''</span> })).<span class="hljs-property">not</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">toBeVisible</span>();

  <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> page.<span class="hljs-title function_">context</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">storageState</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">path</span>: authFile });
});
</code></pre><div>Three things happen here:</div><ol><li><strong>Mock the Supabase endpoints</strong> — intercept <code>/auth/v1/token</code>, <code>/auth/v1/user</code>, <code>/rest/v1/profiles</code> and return canned responses. We don''t hit real Supabase.</li><li><strong>Go through the real login UI</strong> — fill the form, submit. The Supabase client in the browser receives our mocked response and stores the session <strong>exactly the way it would after a real login</strong> (cookies, internal state, whatever Supabase uses).</li><li><strong>Save the storage state to disk</strong> — <code>context().storageState({ path: authFile })</code> dumps cookies and localStorage into a JSON file. Every subsequent test that uses this project starts with this state pre-loaded.</li></ol><div>The flow is the closest thing to a real login without actually hitting Supabase. We''re not faking the storage state — Supabase''s own client builds it for us, we just save what it produced.</div><div><strong>The fake JWT</strong></div><div>Since we mock the entire <code>/auth/v1/token</code> endpoint, the JWT we return doesn''t have to be real — it just has to look like one. In <code>mockAuthenticatedUser</code> we build it manually:</div><div>ts</div><pre data-language="typescript"><code class="hljs language-typescript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> accessToken = [
  <span class="hljs-title function_">toBase64Url</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">JSON</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">stringify</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">alg</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''HS256''</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">typ</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''JWT''</span> })),
  <span class="hljs-title function_">toBase64Url</span>(<span class="hljs-title class_">JSON</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">stringify</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">aud</span>: <span class="hljs-string">''authenticated''</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">sub</span>: user.<span class="hljs-property">id</span>, ... })),
  <span class="hljs-string">''mock-signature''</span>,
].<span class="hljs-title function_">join</span>(<span class="hljs-string">''.''</span>);
</code></pre><div>Three base64 parts separated by dots, real-looking payload with <code>sub</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>exp</code>, and a signature that''s literally the string <code>''mock-signature''</code>. The Supabase client in the browser doesn''t validate signatures — that''s the server''s job. And the server is us. We say it''s valid, it''s valid.</div><div><strong>Test files</strong></div><div>With the setup in place, the actual specs are short and to the point:</div><ul><li><code>admin.spec.ts</code> — single test, verifies that after the admin setup runs, navigating to <code>/posts</code> shows the admin page. This is the smoke test for everything we built — auth, guard, store, the lot. If this passes, the whole chain works.</li><li><code>navbar-authenticated.spec.ts</code> — verifies that on app start with a logged-in user, the navbar renders the authenticated state. Sign Out visible, Sign In gone, "Hello, {username}" rendered.</li><li><code>login-modal.spec.ts</code> — two tests: failed login keeps the modal open and shows an error, logout clears the session and brings back the Sign In button.</li></ul><div>You might ask — why isn''t there a "happy path login" test in <code>login-modal.spec.ts</code>? Because that''s <strong>exactly what the setup project does</strong>. Running it again in a regular spec would be duplication. The setup itself <em>is</em> the happy-path login test — if it fails, no other test in that project even runs.</div><div class="ql-align-center"></div>', false, '2026-08-12T11:09:39.778+00:00', 'https://aqdbdmepncxxuanlymwr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/covers/ChatGPT%20Image%2011%20sie%202026,%2020_53_29.png', '{"0":{"id":"hello-again!","header":2,"content":"Hello again!"},"16":{"id":"supabase-made-the-app-freeze","header":2,"content":"Supabase made the app freeze"},"29":{"id":"my-way-to-handle-supabase-auth","header":2,"content":"My way to handle Supabase Auth"},"34":{"id":"create-client","header":3,"content":"Create Client"},"113":{"id":"initialise-auth","header":3,"content":"Initialise Auth"},"141":{"id":"extract-data-from-supabase","header":3,"content":"Extract data from Supabase"},"220":{"id":"async-admin-guard","header":3,"content":"Async Admin Guard"},"240":{"id":"handling-userprofile","header":3,"content":"Handling UserProfile"},"277":{"id":"bonus:-forcing-the-supabase-client-to-fetch-data-for-server-side-generation","header":3,"content":"Bonus: Forcing the Supabase client to fetch data for server-side generation"},"302":{"id":"tests","header":2,"content":"Tests"},"304":{"id":"unit-tests","header":3,"content":"Unit tests"},"369":{"id":"e2e-tests-(playwright)","header":3,"content":"E2E tests (Playwright)"}}'::jsonb)
  ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

